const e=JSON.parse('[{"path":"skills/SKILL.md","language":"markdown","content":"---\\nname: asciicn\\ndescription: Build ASCII art, ANSI art and text-mode visuals with the asciicn engine (lib/ascii, a WebGL2 glyph shader and Canvas2D grids) in a React or shadcn/ui app. Use when the user wants ASCII, ANSI, Braille, CP437, PETSCII, katakana, halftone glyphs, a terminal or CRT look, an ASCII background, hero, painted landscape or city scene, halftone dots, 3D object, terminal window, meter, card, loader, text effect, or image/video/webcam to ASCII, or a .txt/.ans/.svg/.png/.webm export, or when code imports lib/ascii or components/asciicn.\\n---\\n\\n# asciicn\\n\\nasciicn is an ASCII engine. It draws pictures with real glyphs: a tone from 0 (empty) to 1 (full) becomes a character from a charset, coloured by a colour mode, on a grid of cells that snap to whole device pixels. You build the effect the user asked for on top of it. The asciicn components and backgrounds are examples of what the engine draws, not a menu. Install one only when it is exactly what the user asked for. Otherwise write your own, and borrow technique from the closest example\'s source.\\n\\nThe engine has two renderers that share one set of look props:\\n\\n- **`AsciiShader`** (`@/lib/ascii/shader`, registry item `ascii-shader`): WebGL2. You write a GLSL `field` that returns a tone per sample; the GPU picks glyphs from a glyph atlas. For anything that moves or varies per cell: backgrounds, city skylines, glows, noise, fog, water.\\n- **`AsciiGrid`** (`@/lib/ascii`, registry item `ascii-core`): Canvas2D. You write a `draw` function that fills a frame of glyphs on the CPU, directly or through `mapTones`. For things you compute in JavaScript: 3D meshes, simulations, terminal panes, sampled images and video, text layouts.\\n\\n## 1. Set up the engine\\n\\nFind the engine: `lib/ascii/index.ts`, under the `lib` alias in `components.json` (imports below use `@/lib/ascii`; use the project\'s alias). If it is missing, install it:\\n\\n```bash\\nnpx shadcn@latest add {{REGISTRY_URL}}/ascii-core.json {{REGISTRY_URL}}/ascii-shader.json {{REGISTRY_URL}}/ascii-export.json\\n```\\n\\nThat also installs `ascii-tokens`: the CSS variables `--ascii-ink`, `--ascii-paper`, `--ascii-cell`, `--ascii-font`, `--ascii-glow` and `--ascii-color-<name>`, written into the project\'s global CSS and mapped to the shadcn `--primary` and `--background`. The engine needs React 19 and Tailwind CSS v4.\\n\\nIf `lib/ascii` exists but lacks something this skill names (TypeScript says a prop such as `colorField` or `charset` doesn\'t exist on `AsciiShader`, or `lib/ascii/grid.tsx` is missing), replace the engine. This keeps the project\'s `lib/utils.ts` (`yes n` answers no to every overwrite question):\\n\\n```bash\\nrm -rf lib/ascii && yes n | npx shadcn@latest add {{REGISTRY_URL}}/ascii-core.json {{REGISTRY_URL}}/ascii-shader.json {{REGISTRY_URL}}/ascii-export.json\\n```\\n\\nDon\'t use `--overwrite`: it also replaces `lib/utils.ts`. If the project edited files inside `lib/ascii`, tell the user before replacing them.\\n\\nDone when `lib/ascii/index.ts`, `lib/ascii/grid.tsx` and `lib/ascii/shader.tsx` exist and the global CSS defines `--ascii-ink`.\\n\\n## 2. Pick the technique\\n\\n| The user wants                                                                              | Use                                                                   | Read                                                  |\\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\\n| A background or hero that is a picture: a landscape, a city, a place at a time of day       | `AsciiShader`, `field` + `colorField`, `dots`, `square`, `truecolor`  | [scenes.md](references/scenes.md)                     |\\n| A surface whose tone varies per cell or moves: gradients, noise, fog, water, nebulae, glows | `AsciiShader` with a GLSL `field`                                     | [fields.md](references/fields.md)                     |\\n| Many hues at once: a rainbow, a sunset, a hue per shape                                     | `AsciiShader` with a `field` and a `colorField`, `truecolor` mode     | [fields.md](references/fields.md#colour)              |\\n| Something you compute in JavaScript: a 3D mesh, particles, a simulation, a game of life     | `AsciiGrid` + `createToneField` + `mapTones`                          | [engine-api.md](references/engine-api.md#asciigrid)   |\\n| A waveform, a line of values, a meter or a progress bar                                     | `AsciiGrid` writing glyphs (`eighthsBar`, Braille, `BOX`) or DOM text | [recipes.md](references/recipes.md#a-braille-trace)   |\\n| A terminal pane, boxed panel, status line or log                                            | `AsciiGrid` with `frame.write` and `BOX`, or a `<pre>`                | [recipes.md](references/recipes.md#a-terminal-pane)   |\\n| A photo, logo, video or webcam as ASCII                                                     | `sampleToField` into `mapTones` on an `AsciiGrid`                     | [recipes.md](references/recipes.md#an-image-as-ascii) |\\n| Static ASCII art that must render on the server or in an email                              | `AsciiPre` (a `<pre>` of a frame), or plain text in a `<pre>`         | [engine-api.md](references/engine-api.md#static-art)  |\\n| A file: `.txt`, `.ans`, `.svg`, `.png`, `.webm`                                             | `ascii-export`                                                        | [export.md](references/export.md)                     |\\n\\nThen choose a charset and a colour mode for the picture with [charsets.md](references/charsets.md). The charset is the biggest single decision: Braille for fine lines and wireframes, blocks or quadrants for solid shapes, `standard` or `dense` for photos, katakana for signage, `edge` for outlines, `dots` for halftone pictures. `dots` has five dot sizes (` ·∙•⦁●`); the two smallest are specks, so dark areas can stay quiet. Pair it with the `square` look prop: cells as wide as tall, so the dots lie on an even lattice instead of rows. Braille and quadrants stretch on square cells, so keep `square` for round-glyph ramps.\\n\\nThe examples in [examples.md](references/examples.md) (written when the skill is installed; if it\'s missing, read {{SITE_URL}}/.well-known/agent-skills/asciicn/references/examples.md) are references. If the user asked for exactly one of them, install it. If not, write your own: read the closest example\'s source for its field or draw function, then build what the user described. Never swap the request for an example that is merely nearby, and never guess at an example\'s props; read its docs page or source.\\n\\nDone when every visual in the request has a renderer, a charset and a colour mode.\\n\\n## 3. Build it\\n\\nIf the effect is for a landing page, or a section of one, read [landing-pages.md](references/landing-pages.md) before anything else. If it is a background scene (a place, a landscape, a city), follow [scenes.md](references/scenes.md) step by step and start from its starter scene; it covers framing for phones, the calm centre behind a headline and how to check the result. A good effect on a generic page still looks generated: the page has to be about the product, and the effect has to come from it.\\n\\n1. Read the reference for your technique, and [recipes.md](references/recipes.md) for complete components to adapt.\\n2. Write the component in its own file with `\\"use client\\"` (both renderers are client components; `AsciiPre` renders on the server).\\n3. Give it typed props for what the user would tweak (charset, colour, speed, intensity, value), with defaults that already look good. Pass the engine\'s look props through (`charset`, `chars`, `colorMode`, `phosphor`, `ramp`, `fx`, `cell`, `speed`, `animate`) and accept a `handle` so the frame can be exported.\\n4. Place it with [placement.md](references/placement.md): a renderer fills a positioned parent (`absolute inset-0`), and content sits in a sibling above it.\\n5. Colour it with [theme.md](references/theme.md): token names (`\\"ink\\"`, `\\"blue\\"`, `--chart-1`) so it follows light and dark.\\n\\nDone when the component renders the requested look with no `[ascii-shader]` or WebGL errors in the console.\\n\\n## 4. Check it in a browser\\n\\nOpen the page and look at it. Screenshot it at desktop and phone widths (1440x900 and 390x844; a background scene also behind a sample headline and buttons), in light and dark mode, and once with reduced motion (`prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`). Zoom into a screenshot: you must see individual glyphs. Hold it to [quality.md](references/quality.md).\\n\\nDone when every check in quality.md passes. A screenshot of a flat block, an empty box or blurry glyphs means the field returns the wrong range, the parent has no size, or the cell isn\'t snapped: fix it and look again.\\n\\n## Rules for every effect\\n\\n- Draw glyphs, never pixels. Every visual is characters from a charset (or DOM text). No dots on a canvas, no CSS gradients standing in for glyphs.\\n- A field returns a continuous tone in [0, 1]: 0 empty, 1 the densest glyph. Write smooth math (`smoothstep`, `exp`, `mix`) and let the engine pick glyphs.\\n- `uv` runs 0 to 1 with y up. `res.x / res.y` is the box\'s true aspect ratio, so multiply x by it before measuring distances. Braille samples 2x4 per cell and quadrants 2x2, so never assume one sample per glyph: use `1.0 / res.y` for one sample row.\\n- Paint with theme colours (`\\"ink\\"`, palette names, `--css-variables`). Phosphors, ramps and literal colours are for when the user asks for a terminal look or specific colours.\\n- Text sits on clear ground. Fade the tone toward 0 behind headings, or give the text its own panel. Text contrast stays at 4.5:1 or better.\\n- Motion is slow. Choose `stillTime` so the reduced-motion frame looks finished. Decorative canvases are `aria-hidden`; data visuals have a text equivalent.\\n"},{"path":"skills/references/charsets.md","language":"markdown","content":"# Choosing a charset and a colour mode\\n\\nThe charset decides what the picture is made of; the colour mode decides how it is lit. Choose both for the subject, not by habit.\\n\\n## Charsets\\n\\n| Id          | Samples per cell | Glyphs                                 | Use it for                                                                   | Avoid it for                                 |\\n| ----------- | ---------------- | -------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |\\n| `standard`  | 1x1              | ` .:-=+*#%@`                           | Photos, portraits, classic ASCII art, anything people should read as \\"ASCII\\" | Fine lines (too coarse)                      |\\n| `dense`     | 1x1              | 70 glyphs, `.` to `$`                  | Smooth gradients, faces, fog and clouds                                      | Small cells under 10px (glyphs turn to mush) |\\n| `blocks`    | 1x1              | ` ░▒▓█`                                | Bold fills, bars, ANSI art, light-mode backgrounds                           | Detail                                       |\\n| `quadrants` | 2x2              | ` ▘▝▀▖▌▞▛▗▚▐▜▄▙▟█`                     | Solid shapes at twice the resolution: logos, pixel art, maps, terrain        | Soft gradients                               |\\n| `braille`   | 2x4              | `⠁` to `⣿`                             | Waveforms, wireframes, contour lines, orbits, night skies, fine 3D           | Large filled areas (looks grey)              |\\n| `cp437`     | 1x1              | `·•○◘♠♣♥♦▲▼☺☻░▒▓█`                     | DOS, BBS and retro-PC moods                                                  | Corporate UI                                 |\\n| `petscii`   | 1x1              | `╱╲─│╭╮●◆▄▌█`                          | Commodore 64 moods, playful retro                                            | Photos                                       |\\n| `katakana`  | 1x1              | `ｱｳｴｵｶｷｼﾂｿﾝ` and digits                | Signage, glyph streams                                                       | Anything that should read as calm            |\\n| `dots`      | 1x1              | ` ·∙•⦁●`, five sizes                   | Halftone print, painted scenes and backgrounds (with `square`), soft glows   | Text-like art                                |\\n| `edge`      | 1x1              | `- / \\\\| \\\\` on edges, `standard` inside | Line drawings, outlines of a photo or a 3D shape, blueprints                 | Flat fields with no edges                    |\\n\\n`dots` draws five round dots from geometry, not the font, in radii of 0.07, 0.13, 0.24, 0.33 and 0.42 of the cell. The first two are specks: use tones up to about 0.25 for quiet sky and shadow that reads as air, and save the large dots for light. Set `square` with it, so cells are as wide as tall and the dots lie on an even lattice; on default cells (about twice as tall as wide) the rows sit twice as far apart as the columns and the picture reads as stripes. `square` is for ramps of round glyphs; Braille and quadrants stretch on it. [scenes.md](scenes.md) builds whole pictures this way.\\n\\nCustom ramps: `chars=\\" .oO@\\"` (empty to dense, at least two glyphs) wins over `charset`. Use a custom ramp for a brand (\\"` -=≡`\\" for a minimal look, `\\" 01\\"` for binary). Order glyphs by how much ink they show in the font, or the tones will jump.\\n\\nRules of thumb:\\n\\n- Braille and quadrants only show detail when the field has detail at sample scale. A soft glow in Braille looks like grey static; use `dots` or `standard` for soft things.\\n- On a light background, sparse charsets (`standard`, `dots`, `braille`) look faint. Prefer `blocks`, a larger `cell`, or `invert` for dark-on-light art.\\n- `edge` needs contrast in the field. It is best on images and on fields with clear shapes.\\n- Mixing charsets on one page is fine when each part has its own role: Braille for a waveform, blocks for a progress bar, standard for a hero image.\\n\\n## Colour modes\\n\\n| Mode        | How a glyph is coloured                                                        | Use it for                                                |\\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| `mono`      | Every glyph in `--ascii-ink`, or a `phosphor`                                  | Most UI, terminals, anything that should follow the theme |\\n| `palette`   | The tone picks a colour from `palette` (paper, then inks from faint to strong) | Theme-coloured backgrounds with depth, one hue per line   |\\n| `duotone`   | The tone picks a colour on a `ramp`, dark to bright                            | Moody backgrounds, heat, night skies                      |\\n| `truecolor` | The source colour per glyph: a `colorField`, an image, a video                 | Photos, video, sunsets, neon, anything with real colour   |\\n| `ansi16`    | The source colour snapped to 16 terminal colours (`ansi=\\"vga\\"` or `\\"xterm\\"`)   | ANSI art, terminal screenshots, `.ans` export             |\\n\\nPhosphors for `mono`: `p1` green `#33ff66`, `p3` amber `#ffb000`, `ibm` white `#f4f4f5`, `blueprint` cyan `#38bdf8`. They are fixed colours, so give them a dark paper in light mode (wrap the section in `className=\\"dark\\"` or set `paper`).\\n\\nDuotone ramps: `mainframe`, `sodium`, `heliograph`, `infrared`, `outrun`, `orchid`, `glacier`, `ember`, `toxic`, `vapor`. Read `RAMPS` in `lib/ascii/palettes.ts` for the stops, or pass your own: `ramp={[\\"#0b1020\\", \\"#3b82f6\\", \\"#e0f2fe\\"]}`.\\n\\n## Grading\\n\\n`grade={{ brightness, contrast, gamma, saturation }}` adjusts tones before the glyph is chosen. Raise `contrast` (1.2 to 1.6) when a field or image uses only a few glyphs. Lower `gamma` (0.7 to 0.9) to open up shadows in a photo. `invert` swaps dense and empty, for dark ink on a light page.\\n\\n`dither=\\"bayer4\\"` (or `\\"bayer8\\"`, `\\"blue-noise\\"`) blends neighbouring glyph levels in a fixed pattern. It smooths gradients in short ramps such as `blocks` and `dots`.\\n\\n## Picking for common requests\\n\\n| Request                               | Charset               | Colour mode                             |\\n| ------------------------------------- | --------------------- | --------------------------------------- |\\n| A terminal or CRT panel               | `standard` + text     | `mono` with `p1` or `p3`, `fx={CRT_FX}` |\\n| A photo turned to ASCII               | `standard` or `dense` | `truecolor`, or `mono` for print        |\\n| A waveform or a line of values        | `braille`             | `mono` or `palette` per line            |\\n| A density grid or level bars          | `blocks`              | `palette`                               |\\n| A logo in ASCII                       | `quadrants`           | `mono`                                  |\\n| A night sky, orbit or wireframe globe | `braille`             | `mono` or `duotone`                     |\\n| Fire, a nebula, a sunset              | `dense` or `blocks`   | `truecolor` with a `colorField`         |\\n| A blueprint or technical drawing      | `edge`                | `mono` with `blueprint`                 |\\n| Newsprint or a halftone portrait      | `dots`                | `mono`, `invert` on light pages         |\\n| A painted landscape or city scene     | `dots` with `square`  | `truecolor` with a `colorField`         |\\n| Retro game or BBS screen              | `cp437` or `petscii`  | `ansi16`                                |\\n"},{"path":"skills/references/engine-api.md","language":"markdown","content":"# Engine API\\n\\nThe engine installs as plain source. When this file and the code disagree, the code wins: read `lib/ascii/index.ts` and the file named in each section.\\n\\n- `@/lib/ascii` (item `ascii-core`): CPU side. Charsets, palettes, frames, cell metrics, `AsciiGrid`, `mapTones`, theme and motion hooks. No WebGL.\\n- `@/lib/ascii/shader` (item `ascii-shader`): `AsciiShader`, the WebGL2 glyph renderer.\\n- `@/lib/ascii/export` (item `ascii-export`): files. See [export.md](export.md).\\n\\n## Look props\\n\\nBoth renderers, and every component and background, take the same look props (`AsciiLook` in `lib/ascii/look.ts`):\\n\\n| Prop            | Type                                                          | What it does                                                                                                                                    |\\n| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| `charset`       | `CharsetName`                                                 | Which glyphs draw the tones. See [charsets.md](charsets.md)                                                                                     |\\n| `chars`         | `string`                                                      | A custom ramp from empty to dense, such as `\\" .oO@\\"`. Wins over `charset`                                                                       |\\n| `colorMode`     | `\\"mono\\" \\\\| \\"truecolor\\" \\\\| \\"ansi16\\" \\\\| \\"duotone\\" \\\\| \\"palette\\"` | How glyphs are coloured                                                                                                                         |\\n| `phosphor`      | `\\"p1\\" \\\\| \\"p3\\" \\\\| \\"ibm\\" \\\\| \\"blueprint\\"`                        | `mono`: a CRT screen colour instead of `--ascii-ink`                                                                                            |\\n| `ramp`          | `RampName \\\\| string[]`                                        | `duotone`: a named ramp or `#rrggbb` stops, dark to bright                                                                                      |\\n| `ansi`          | `\\"vga\\" \\\\| \\"xterm\\"`                                            | `ansi16`: which 16-colour table. Default `\\"vga\\"`                                                                                                |\\n| `grade`         | `{ brightness, contrast, gamma, saturation }`                 | Tone grading before the glyph is chosen                                                                                                         |\\n| `invert`        | `boolean`                                                     | Dense glyphs for dark tones. Use for dark-on-light art                                                                                          |\\n| `threshold`     | `number`                                                      | Braille and quadrants: the tone that lights a dot. Default 0.5                                                                                  |\\n| `edgeThreshold` | `number`                                                      | `edge`: Sobel strength that draws `- / \\\\| \\\\`. Default 0.22                                                                                      |\\n| `dither`        | `\\"none\\" \\\\| \\"bayer2\\" \\\\| \\"bayer4\\" \\\\| \\"bayer8\\" \\\\| \\"blue-noise\\"`  | Ordered dithering between glyph levels, for smooth gradients with few glyphs                                                                    |\\n| `background`    | `\\"paper\\" \\\\| \\"transparent\\"`                                    | `\\"transparent\\"` draws glyphs only, so the page shows between them                                                                               |\\n| `square`        | `boolean`                                                     | Cells as wide as tall (`cell` sets both sides), so `dots` lies on an even lattice. Braille and quadrants stretch on it. Default `false`         |\\n| `fx`            | `PostFx`                                                      | CRT effects, each 0 to 1: `scanlines`, `bloom`, `curvature`, `glitch`, `flicker`, `vignette`. `CRT_FX` is a full preset, `NO_FX` turns them off |\\n\\nShared runtime props: `cell` (glyph cell height in CSS px, default `--ascii-cell`), `speed` (time multiplier, default 1), `animate` (`false` holds a still frame), `fps` (cap), `stillTime` (seconds drawn when motion is off or reduced), `fallback` (shown until the first frame) and `handle` (a ref filled with an `AsciiHandle` for export).\\n\\n## AsciiShader\\n\\n`lib/ascii/shader.tsx`. A client component that fills its positioned parent.\\n\\n```tsx\\n\\"use client\\";\\n\\nimport { AsciiShader } from \\"@/lib/ascii/shader\\";\\n\\nconst FIELD = /* glsl */ `\\nfloat field(vec2 uv, vec2 res, float t) {\\n  vec2 p = (uv - 0.5) * vec2(res.x / res.y, 1.0);\\n  return smoothstep(0.45, 0.0, length(p)) * (0.75 + 0.25 * sin(t));\\n}`;\\n\\nexport const Glow = () => (\\n  <AsciiShader field={FIELD} charset=\\"braille\\" className=\\"absolute inset-0\\" />\\n);\\n```\\n\\n| Prop           | What it does                                                                                                    |\\n| -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| `field`        | GLSL: `float field(vec2 uv, vec2 res, float t)` returning a tone in [0, 1]. See [fields.md](fields.md)          |\\n| `colorField`   | GLSL: `vec3 colorField(vec2 uv, vec2 res, float t)` (or `vec4`) returning RGB 0..1 for `truecolor` and `ansi16` |\\n| `uniforms`     | `{ name: number \\\\| [x, y] \\\\| [x, y, z] \\\\| [x, y, z, w] }`, declared for you. Names must not start with `u_`     |\\n| `palette`      | Paper then inks, as token names or CSS colours. Used by `colorMode=\\"palette\\"`                                   |\\n| `ink`, `paper` | Mono overrides                                                                                                  |\\n| `interaction`  | `\\"none\\" \\\\| \\"repel\\" \\\\| \\"ripple\\" \\\\| \\"trail\\"`: what the pointer does to the field                                  |\\n| `audio`        | Audio bands from `useAudioBands` (`lib/ascii/audio.ts`), read as `u_audio`, or `null`                           |\\n| `seed`         | A number read as `u_seed`, for seeded variations. Default 0                                                     |\\n| look props     | All of the table above                                                                                          |\\n\\nDefault `colorMode` is `truecolor` when `colorField` is set, otherwise `palette` (paper to ink). Keep the `field` string a module-level constant and pass changing values through `uniforms`; a new string recompiles the shader.\\n\\n`data-state` on the root reports `running`, `paused` (offscreen or hidden tab), `static` (reduced motion or `animate={false}`) or `fallback` (no WebGL2, or a compile error, which is logged with the line).\\n\\n## AsciiGrid\\n\\n`lib/ascii/grid.tsx`. A Canvas2D grid of glyphs drawn by a function you write, at device resolution, theme-aware, paused offscreen, one still frame under reduced motion.\\n\\n```tsx\\n\\"use client\\";\\n\\nimport { AsciiGrid } from \\"@/lib/ascii\\";\\nimport type { AsciiGridDrawContext } from \\"@/lib/ascii\\";\\n\\nconst draw = ({ frame, columns, rows, time }: AsciiGridDrawContext) => {\\n  const message = \\"SYSTEM READY\\";\\n  const x = Math.floor((columns - message.length) / 2);\\n  frame.write(x, Math.floor(rows / 2), message);\\n  frame.put(\\n    x + message.length + 1,\\n    Math.floor(rows / 2),\\n    time % 1 < 0.5 ? \\"█\\" : \\" \\"\\n  );\\n};\\n\\nexport const Ready = () => (\\n  <AsciiGrid draw={draw} className=\\"absolute inset-0\\" />\\n);\\n```\\n\\n`draw(ctx)` runs every frame while animating, once for a still frame, and again on resize. `ctx` holds:\\n\\n- `frame`: a `MutableFrame`, cleared to spaces before each draw. `put(x, y, glyph, rgba?)`, `write(x, y, text, rgba?)`, `fillRect(x, y, w, h, glyph, rgba?)`, `get(x, y)`. Out-of-bounds writes are ignored, so you never need to clip. Row 0 is the top.\\n- `columns`, `rows`: the grid size for the current box and cell.\\n- `time` (seconds, scaled by `speed`), `delta` (seconds since the last draw, 0 when still).\\n- `pointer`: `{ x, y, inside, down }` in cell units, when `interactive` is set.\\n- `metrics`: the measured `CellMetrics`.\\n\\nProps: `draw`, `cell`, `ink`, `paper` (default `\\"transparent\\"`), `colored` (per-glyph colour; set it when you pass `rgba` or use a colour mode), `speed`, `animate`, `fps` (default 30), `stillTime`, `interactive`, `fallback`, `handle`.\\n\\n### Tones to glyphs on the CPU\\n\\nFor anything with a tone per point (3D shading, a heightmap, a simulation), fill a `ToneField` and let `mapTones` choose glyphs. It handles every charset, sub-cell sampling and colour mode the same way the GPU does.\\n\\n```ts\\nimport {\\n  createToneField,\\n  fillToneField,\\n  mapTones,\\n  resolveCharset,\\n} from \\"@/lib/ascii\\";\\n\\n// Inside draw:\\nconst charset = resolveCharset(\\"braille\\");\\nconst field = createToneField(columns, rows, charset); // columns*2 by rows*4 samples for Braille\\nfillToneField(field, (u, v) =>\\n  /* u, v in [0, 1], v = 0 at the TOP */ Math.max(\\n    0,\\n    1 - Math.hypot(u - 0.5, v - 0.5) * 2\\n  )\\n);\\nmapTones(field, columns, rows, { charset, colorMode: \\"mono\\" }, frame);\\n```\\n\\n`fillToneField`\'s callback may return a tone or `[tone, r, g, b]` (0..255) when the field was created with `{ color: true }`. `mapTones(field, columns, rows, options, into?)` writes into `into` (pass `ctx.frame`) and takes `charset`, `chars`, `grade`, `invert`, `threshold`, `edgeThreshold`, `dither`, `colorMode`, `ramp`, `ansi` and `palette` (paper to ink, as `[r, g, b]` bytes).\\n\\nReuse the field across frames: create it once per size (keep it in a ref and recreate when `columns` or `rows` change), and refill it each frame.\\n\\n## Charset helpers\\n\\n`lib/ascii/charsets.ts`:\\n\\n- `CHARSETS`, `CHARSET_NAMES`, `resolveCharset(charset, chars?)` returns `{ id, label, kind, glyphs, grid }`. `kind` is `\\"ramp\\"`, `\\"braille\\"`, `\\"quadrants\\"` or `\\"edge\\"`; `grid` is the samples per cell (`[2, 4]` for Braille).\\n- `rampChar(charset, tone)`: the glyph for a tone on a ramp charset.\\n- `brailleChar(mask)` with `BRAILLE_DOT_BITS[row][col]` (row 0..3, col 0..1): build a Braille glyph dot by dot, for traces, waveforms and plots.\\n- `quadrantChar(mask)`, `QUADRANT_GLYPHS`; `edgeChar(gx, gy)` (y down) picks `-`, `/`, `|` or `\\\\`.\\n- `EIGHTHS_UP` (` ▁▂▃▄▅▆▇█`) and `EIGHTHS_RIGHT` (` ▏▎▍▌▋▊▉█`): bars with eighth-cell precision. `eighthsBar(value, width)` returns a horizontal bar string for `value` in [0, 1].\\n- `SHADES` (` ░▒▓█`), `BOX` (`ascii`, `light`, `heavy`, `double`, `rounded`, each with `tl tr bl br h v x`), `SPINNERS`.\\n\\n## Colour helpers\\n\\n`lib/ascii/palettes.ts` and `lib/ascii/color.ts`:\\n\\n- `PHOSPHORS`, `RAMPS`, `ANSI16.vga`, `ANSI16.xterm`, `sampleRamp(stops, t)`, `hexToRgb`, `rgbToHex`, `nearestIndex(rgb, palette)` (OKLab distance), `luma`, `gradeTone`, `gradeRgb`.\\n- `cssColor(name)`: `\\"green\\"` to `var(--ascii-color-green, …)`, `\\"--chart-1\\"` to `var(--chart-1)`.\\n- `useAsciiColors(ref, names?)`: resolves token names (default paper and ink) to concrete RGBA on the client and re-renders when the theme changes. Returns `null` until mounted. `resolveColor(css)`, `subscribeToTheme(callback)` for non-React code.\\n- `seriesColor(color, i)`, `SERIES_COLORS`: `color` if set, otherwise the i-th named colour in a fixed order, for telling several lines apart.\\n\\n## Cell metrics\\n\\n`lib/ascii/measure.ts`: `measureCell({ cell, font, dpr })` returns `{ width, height, deviceWidth, deviceHeight, dpr, fontSize, font }`. The cell height is `cell`; the width is the font\'s advance; both snap to whole device pixels so glyphs stay sharp. `gridSize(metrics, width, height)` gives `{ columns, rows }`. `readCell(el)` and `readFont(el)` read `--ascii-cell` and `--ascii-font`. `DEFAULT_CELL` is 12.\\n\\n## Motion hooks\\n\\n`lib/ascii/motion.ts`: `useFrameLoop((time, delta) => …, { target, enabled, fps, stillTime })` drives your own loop with the same rules as the renderers (pause offscreen and in hidden tabs, one frame under reduced motion). `usePrefersReducedMotion()`, `useInView(ref)`, `useDocumentVisible()`.\\n\\n## Static art\\n\\n`AsciiPre` (`lib/ascii/pre.tsx`) renders an `AsciiFrame` as a `<pre>`, with colour runs as spans. It works in server components, emails and RSS. For a fixed banner, a plain `<pre aria-label=\\"…\\">` with the text is fine too. `frameToText(frame)` and `frameLines(frame)` turn a frame into strings.\\n\\nFor a CPU renderer that needs the CRT look, `CrtOverlay` (`lib/ascii/crt.tsx`) approximates `fx` in CSS over a canvas or DOM text.\\n\\n## Sampling images and video\\n\\n`sampleToField(source, { columns, rows, charset, fit, mirror, crop, aspect, into })` (`lib/ascii/sample.ts`) reads an image, video, canvas or webcam frame into a `ToneField` with colour, area-averaged per sample. `autoLevels(field)` stretches its tones to the full range. `useMediaElement(urlOrBlobOrStream)` loads an `<img>` or `<video>` and returns `{ element, kind, error }`. Then `mapTones` as above. [recipes.md](recipes.md#an-image-as-ascii) has the full component.\\n"},{"path":"skills/references/export.md","language":"markdown","content":"# Export\\n\\n`ascii-export` (`lib/ascii/export.ts`) turns what a renderer drew into files. When this file and the code disagree, the code wins.\\n\\n## Get the frame\\n\\nEvery renderer and component takes a `handle` ref. The engine fills it with an `AsciiHandle`:\\n\\n```tsx\\n\\"use client\\";\\n\\nimport { useRef } from \\"react\\";\\n\\nimport type { AsciiHandle } from \\"@/lib/ascii\\";\\nimport { copyText, toText } from \\"@/lib/ascii/export\\";\\n\\nexport const Exportable = () => {\\n  const handle = useRef<AsciiHandle | null>(null);\\n  const copy = () => {\\n    const frame = handle.current?.capture();\\n    if (frame) {\\n      void copyText(toText(frame, { trimEnd: true }));\\n    }\\n  };\\n  return (\\n    <div className=\\"relative h-80\\">\\n      <MyBackground handle={handle} className=\\"absolute inset-0\\" />\\n      <button type=\\"button\\" onClick={copy} className=\\"relative\\">\\n        Copy as text\\n      </button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n};\\n```\\n\\n`handle.current` has `capture()` (the current `AsciiFrame`: `columns`, `rows`, `glyphs`, `colors`), `canvas`, `colors()` (the resolved ink and paper) and `metrics()` (the `CellMetrics`). The GPU renderer reads back the glyphs it drew, so exported text is exactly what is on screen.\\n\\n## Formats\\n\\n| Function                                                          | Returns            | Notes                                                                                                                                                   |\\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| `toText(frame, { trimEnd })`                                      | `string`           | Plain text, one line per row                                                                                                                            |\\n| `toAnsi(frame, { mode, ansi, ink, paper, background })`           | `string`           | ANSI escape codes. `mode: \\"truecolor\\"` (24-bit, default) or `\\"ansi16\\"` (snapped to `ansi`: `vga` or `xterm`). Save as `.ans`, or `cat` it in a terminal |\\n| `toSvg(frame, { metrics, ink, paper, font, shapes })`             | `string`           | Blocks, shades, Braille, box lines and dots become vector shapes (`shapes`, default true); other glyphs are text                                        |\\n| `toPng(handleOrFrameOrCanvas, { scale, ink, paper, cell, font })` | `Promise<Blob>`    | A handle without `scale` saves the live canvas, CRT effects included; otherwise the frame is re-rendered                                                |\\n| `recordCanvas(canvas, { durationMs, fps, mimeType })`             | `Promise<Blob>`    | Records the live canvas. `createRecorder(canvas, options)` gives a start/stop recorder for a button                                                     |\\n| `pickVideoType()`, `videoExtension(type)`                         | `string`           | The first video type the browser can record (WebM first, MP4 in Safari) and its extension                                                               |\\n| `downloadBlob(blob, name)`, `downloadText(text, name)`            | `void`             | Saves a file                                                                                                                                            |\\n| `copyText(text)`                                                  | `Promise<boolean>` | Clipboard; resolves `false` when the browser refuses                                                                                                    |\\n\\n`handle.current.colors()` returns the ink and paper on screen as CSS colours; pass them to `toSvg` and `toPng` so the file matches the theme. `toAnsi` needs `#rrggbb` strings or `[r, g, b]` bytes: convert a CSS colour with `resolveColor` (RGBA from 0 to 1) and scale by 255.\\n\\n## Static art on the server\\n\\n`frameToText(frame)` and `AsciiPre` work without a browser, so a frame computed with `createToneField` and `mapTones` can be rendered into a server component, an email, a README or an RSS feed.\\n\\n## Checks\\n\\n- Export from a frame that has finished drawing: call `capture()` after the first frame (the root\'s `data-state` is no longer `fallback`).\\n- Recording needs `MediaRecorder` and `canvas.captureStream`. Show the record button only when `pickVideoType()` returns a type, and name the file with `videoExtension(type)`: Safari may record MP4 instead of WebM.\\n- `.ans` files read correctly in terminals that support the chosen mode. Use `ansi16` for maximum compatibility.\\n"},{"path":"skills/references/fields.md","language":"markdown","content":"# Writing a field\\n\\nA field is the GLSL function you write for `AsciiShader`. It returns a tone for one sample point; the engine grades it, picks the glyph from the charset, colours it and draws it from a glyph atlas.\\n\\n```glsl\\nfloat field(vec2 uv, vec2 res, float t) {\\n  // uv:  the sample centre, 0 to 1 on both axes, (0, 0) bottom left, y up\\n  // res: the sample grid, measured in sample rows (see below)\\n  // t:   seconds of motion, already multiplied by `speed`\\n  return uv.y; // 0 = empty cell, 1 = the densest glyph\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe return value is clamped to [0, 1]. With a ramp charset (`standard`, `dense`, `blocks`, `cp437`…), the tone picks a glyph by ink coverage: 0.1 is a `.`, 0.9 is a `#` or `@`. With `braille` and `quadrants`, each dot or quadrant lights where its own sample is above `threshold`. With `edge`, the engine runs a Sobel filter over your tones and draws `- / | \\\\` along edges.\\n\\n## The `res` contract\\n\\nThe field is sampled on a grid finer than the glyph grid when the charset packs several dots into one glyph:\\n\\n| Charset       | Samples per cell |\\n| ------------- | ---------------- |\\n| Ramps, `edge` | 1 x 1            |\\n| `quadrants`   | 2 x 2            |\\n| `braille`     | 2 x 4            |\\n\\n`res.y` is the number of sample rows. `res.x` is `res.y * boxWidth / boxHeight`, so `res.x / res.y` is always the box\'s true aspect ratio, even though a glyph cell is about half as wide as it is tall. Two consequences:\\n\\n- Measure distances in square units: `vec2 p = vec2(uv.x * res.x / res.y, uv.y);`. Circles stay round in every charset.\\n- `1.0 / res.y` is one sample row. Use it for line widths and finite differences. Don\'t use `1.0 / res.x` as \\"one cell\\", and don\'t assume one sample per glyph: a line one sample wide is a quarter of a Braille cell.\\n\\nWrite the field once and it works in every charset. Test it in `standard` and in `braille`: if it only looks right in one, it assumes a sample size.\\n\\n## The rules of the language\\n\\nFields are GLSL ES 1.00 (the field pass runs on WebGL2, but in the 1.00 dialect).\\n\\n- Every literal used as a float needs a decimal point: `2.0`, not `2`. `int` and `float` never mix without a cast: `float(i)`.\\n- Loops need constant bounds: `for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)`. Break early with `if (float(i) >= count) break;` when the count is a uniform.\\n- No arrays in uniforms. Pass up to four numbers per uniform (`vec4`) and use several uniforms.\\n- Write helpers above `field` in the same string, prefixed with your component\'s name (`topoHeight`, `waveCrest`) so they never collide.\\n- Reserved: uniforms starting with `u_`, helpers starting with `ascii_`, and the names `field`, `colorField`, `main`, `hash21`, `snoise`, `fbm`, `spectrum`, `paletteColor`, `oklch`, `toSrgb`, `aces`.\\n- GLSL ES 1.00 keeps words it never uses, and none compile as names: `input`, `output`, `half`, `fixed`, `long`, `short`, `double`, `unsigned`, `public`, `static`, `extern`, `external`, `class`, `union`, `enum`, `typedef`, `template`, `goto`, `inline`, `noinline`, `volatile`, `cast`, `sizeof`, `this`, `packed`, `default`, `switch`, `interface`, `namespace`, `using`, `asm`, `flat`, `superp`, and types such as `hvec2`, `dvec3`, `fvec4` or `sampler3D`. Names starting with `gl_` or `webgl_`, or containing `__`, are reserved too. Built-in names (`dot`, `step`, `mix`, `length`, `distance`, `noise`) can\'t be variables either. Prefixed names avoid all of them.\\n- A broken field logs a compile error with the line, and the component shows its fallback. Read the console, fix the GLSL, reload.\\n\\n## Built-in helpers\\n\\n| Helper                                  | Returns                                                                                     |\\n| --------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| `float hash21(vec2 p)`                  | A stable pseudo-random value in [0, 1). `hash21(floor(p))` gives one value per grid square. |\\n| `float snoise(vec2 p)`                  | 2D simplex noise in [-1, 1], feature size about 1 unit of `p`.                              |\\n| `float fbm(vec2 p)`                     | Four octaves of simplex noise, in [0, 1], mean 0.5.                                         |\\n| `vec3 spectrum(float h)`                | A saturated rainbow, red at 0 and 1. Wraps.                                                 |\\n| `vec4 paletteColor(float x)`            | The palette\'s ink colours (every entry after paper) as a ramp from 0 to 1.                  |\\n| `vec3 oklch(float L, float C, float h)` | An OKLCH colour, `h` in radians, as linear RGB. Pass it through `toSrgb` before returning.  |\\n| `vec3 toSrgb(vec3 linear)`              | Linear RGB to sRGB, clamped.                                                                |\\n| `vec3 aces(vec3 x)`                     | ACES filmic tone map, for bright, saturated colour that shouldn\'t clip.                     |\\n\\n## Built-in uniforms\\n\\n| Uniform             | Type  | Value                                                                                        |\\n| ------------------- | ----- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| `u_time`            | float | Same as `t`                                                                                  |\\n| `u_resolution`      | vec2  | Same as `res`                                                                                |\\n| `u_pointer`         | vec3  | Pointer `xy` in `uv` space, `z` = presence from 0 to 1, smoothed. 0 when the pointer is away |\\n| `u_pointerVelocity` | vec2  | Pointer velocity in `uv` per second                                                          |\\n| `u_click`           | vec3  | Last press position `xy`, `z` = seconds since it                                             |\\n| `u_audio`           | vec4  | Bass, mid, treble, level, each 0 to 1, from the `audio` prop (`useAudioBands`)               |\\n| `u_seed`            | float | The `seed` prop (default 0). Offset noise by it so two copies of a component can differ      |\\n\\nRead `u_pointer` for parallax or steering: `vec2 look = (u_pointer.xy - 0.5) * u_pointer.z;`. For push, ripple and trail effects you don\'t need to write anything: set `interaction=\\"repel\\"`, `\\"ripple\\"` or `\\"trail\\"` and the engine applies them to your tones.\\n\\n## Your own uniforms\\n\\nPass anything the user should control as a uniform, never by rebuilding the string:\\n\\n```tsx\\n<AsciiShader\\n  field={FIELD}\\n  uniforms={{ topoScale: scale, topoLines: lines, topoFocus: [0.7, 0.4] }}\\n/>\\n```\\n\\n`topoScale` becomes `uniform float topoScale;`, `topoFocus` becomes `uniform vec2 topoFocus;`. They are declared for you; declaring them yourself is a compile error.\\n\\n## Coordinates\\n\\n```glsl\\nfloat aspect = res.x / res.y;\\nvec2 p = vec2(uv.x * aspect, uv.y);         // x 0..aspect, y 0..1, square units\\nvec2 c = (uv - 0.5) * vec2(aspect, 1.0);    // centred: (0, 0) in the middle, y -0.5..0.5\\n```\\n\\nA point given from the top left, as CSS does (`[x, y]` with y down), converts with `vec2(x * aspect, 1.0 - y)`. Rotate with `mat2(cos(a), -sin(a), sin(a), cos(a)) * c`. To keep a pattern the same size in cells whatever the box size, scale by `res.y`: `p * res.y / 40.0` is one unit per 40 sample rows.\\n\\n## Building blocks\\n\\nEach returns a tone in [0, 1]. Combine with `max` (union), `min` (intersection), `*` (mask), `mix` (blend).\\n\\n### Gradients and light\\n\\n```glsl\\nfloat linear = uv.y;\\nfloat radial = 1.0 - smoothstep(0.0, 0.6, length(c));\\nfloat glow = exp(-dot(c, c) * 8.0);                 // larger number = tighter\\nfloat edgeFade = smoothstep(0.0, 0.35, uv.y);       // clear the bottom edge\\n```\\n\\nGlyph ramps are coarse (10 levels in `standard`), so wide soft gradients band. Set `dither=\\"bayer4\\"` to blend neighbouring glyph levels, or use `dense` (70 levels).\\n\\n### Noise\\n\\n```glsl\\nfloat clouds = fbm(p * 3.0 + vec2(t * 0.04, 0.0));\\nfloat ridges = 1.0 - abs(snoise(p * 2.5));\\nfloat warped = fbm(p * 2.0 + 0.6 * vec2(fbm(p * 1.5 + t * 0.03), fbm(p * 1.5 - t * 0.02)));\\n```\\n\\n### Lines\\n\\nThin lines read best in `braille` (eight dots per glyph). Measure the distance to the line in sample rows, then turn it into a tone that is 1 on the line and 0 a sample or two away:\\n\\n```glsl\\nfloat px = 1.0 / res.y;                                  // one sample row\\nfloat ring = 1.0 - smoothstep(0.0, 1.5 * px, abs(length(c) - 0.3));\\nfloat wave = 1.0 - smoothstep(0.0, 1.5 * px, abs(p.y - 0.5 - 0.1 * sin(p.x * 6.0 + t)));\\n```\\n\\nContour lines of a height function `h(p)`, about `width` samples wide:\\n\\n```glsl\\nfloat topoLine(float h, float hx, float hy, float count, float width) {\\n  float v = h * count;\\n  float offset = abs(fract(v + 0.5) - 0.5);\\n  float perSample = max(length(vec2(hx - h, hy - h)) * count, 1e-4);\\n  float d = offset / perSample;\\n  return (1.0 - smoothstep(width * 0.5 - 0.5, width * 0.5 + 0.5, d)) * (1.0 - smoothstep(0.06, 0.1, offset));\\n}\\n// float h = topoHeight(p);\\n// float hx = topoHeight(p + vec2(1.0 / res.y, 0.0));\\n// float hy = topoHeight(p + vec2(0.0, 1.0 / res.y));\\n// return topoLine(h, hx, hy, 12.0, 1.5);\\n```\\n\\nUse a smooth height for contour lines (one or two low-frequency `snoise` layers); raw high-frequency `fbm` breaks lines into dots.\\n\\n### Shapes and shading\\n\\nA flat 1.0 inside a shape draws a solid block of `@`. Shade it so the glyphs vary:\\n\\n```glsl\\nfloat disc = 1.0 - smoothstep(r - 0.01, r + 0.01, length(c));\\nfloat lit = disc * mix(0.3, 1.0, smoothstep(-0.3, 0.3, c.y + c.x * 0.4));   // lit from the top right\\nfloat rim = disc * smoothstep(r * 0.6, r, length(c));                       // denser at the edge\\n```\\n\\nFor a sphere or a 3D look, compute a normal and a light (`max(dot(n, light), 0.0)`) and return the lambert term. Glyph ramps read shading very well; that is what ASCII art is good at.\\n\\n### Columns\\n\\nPer-column motion uses the glyph grid, not the sample grid. Step in whole cells: `floor(uv.x * columns)`, where `columns` is `res.x` divided by the charset\'s samples per cell across (1 for ramps). Pass `columns` as a uniform when the charset can change, or keep per-column effects on a ramp charset such as `katakana`.\\n\\n## Motion\\n\\n- Drift by adding `t * speed` to coordinates: speeds of 0.01 to 0.08 units per second read as calm.\\n- Pulse with `1.0 + 0.05 * sin(t * 0.4)`.\\n- Avoid `hash21` of anything with `t` in it: every glyph changes every frame and the effect flickers. For glyphs that change on purpose (falling glyphs, glitches), quantise time first: `hash21(vec2(column, floor(t * 8.0)))`.\\n- `stillTime` sets the frame shown under reduced motion. Pick one where the composition looks finished.\\n\\n## Colour\\n\\n`colorField` is an optional second function. It returns the colour of the glyph in a cell; `field` still decides which glyph. Setting it makes `truecolor` the default colour mode, and `ansi16` snaps its output to 16 terminal colours.\\n\\n```tsx\\nconst FIELD = /* glsl */ `\\nfloat field(vec2 uv, vec2 res, float t) {\\n  vec2 c = (uv - 0.5) * vec2(res.x / res.y, 1.0);\\n  return exp(-pow(abs(length(c) - 0.3), 2.0) * 900.0);\\n}`;\\n\\nconst COLOR_FIELD = /* glsl */ `\\nvec3 colorField(vec2 uv, vec2 res, float t) {\\n  vec2 c = (uv - 0.5) * vec2(res.x / res.y, 1.0);\\n  return toSrgb(oklch(0.75, 0.15, atan(c.y, c.x) + t * 0.3));\\n}`;\\n\\n<AsciiShader\\n  field={FIELD}\\n  colorField={COLOR_FIELD}\\n  charset=\\"braille\\"\\n  className=\\"absolute inset-0\\"\\n/>;\\n```\\n\\n- Same arguments and uniforms as `field`. Return `vec3` RGB or `vec4` RGBA in [0, 1].\\n- `field` and `colorField` compile into one shader. Shared geometry goes in a helper; the engine keeps one copy of a helper that appears word for word in both strings.\\n- `oklch` gives even, controllable hues; `spectrum` is fully saturated and goes thin on white (yellow and cyan vanish). Mix in theme ink for light mode: `mix(spectrum(h), paletteColor(0.0).rgb, 0.25)`.\\n- Without `colorField`, `palette` mode maps tone to the palette and `duotone` to the ramp.\\n- To paint a whole picture (a landscape or a city), paint the colour first and read the tone back from it, in one cached function both fields call. [scenes.md](scenes.md) shows how.\\n\\n| Colour                            | GLSL body                                                                                     |\\n| --------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| Hue across the box                | `return toSrgb(oklch(0.72, 0.14, uv.x * 6.2832));`                                            |\\n| Hue around a centre               | `return spectrum(atan(c.y, c.x) / 6.2832);`                                                   |\\n| Theme colours along x             | `return paletteColor(uv.x).rgb;` with `palette={[\\"--ascii-paper\\", \\"--chart-1\\", \\"--chart-2\\"]}` |\\n| Fire: dark red to white by height | `return aces(vec3(3.0, 1.2, 0.4) * tone);` with the tone from a shared helper                 |\\n| Fade out instead of thinning      | `return vec4(spectrum(uv.x), smoothstep(0.0, 0.3, uv.y));`                                    |\\n\\n## Tuning the look\\n\\n- Too busy behind text: multiply the tone (`* 0.6`) or clear it under the text with `smoothstep` on `uv`.\\n- Glyphs too uniform: widen the tone range. A field that sits between 0.4 and 0.6 draws one or two glyphs; stretch it with `smoothstep(0.3, 0.7, x)` or `grade={{ contrast: 1.4 }}`.\\n- Too dark in light mode: sparse charsets read faint on white. Try `blocks`, a bigger `cell`, or `invert`.\\n- Want a bolder look: `cell={16}` to `cell={20}`. Fine texture: 10 to 12.\\n"},{"path":"skills/references/landing-pages.md","language":"markdown","content":"# Designing a landing page around an ASCII effect\\n\\nA working ASCII effect on a generic page still reads as generated. The effect has to be the page\'s identity, drawn from what the product is, and the page around it has to be specific, quiet and true. Do this before you write a field.\\n\\n## 1. Know the product\\n\\nThe page is for the product in this repo, not for asciicn. asciicn is the tool you build with: don\'t name it on the page, and don\'t make the page about ASCII art.\\n\\nRead the README, `package.json`, the existing pages and any copy in the repo. Write yourself a short brief:\\n\\n- What the product is and who it is for, in one sentence, in the product\'s own words.\\n- The one thing a visitor should do (install, sign up, download, book).\\n- The facts you can use: features, platforms, prices, numbers. Only facts that are in the repo or the user\'s message.\\n\\nIf there is no product to read about, ask the user what the page is for. If nobody can answer, pick something plausible, keep it modest, and say what you picked.\\n\\n## 2. Find the effect in the subject\\n\\nThe hero effect should be something only this product would show. Take it from the product\'s own world and render it in glyphs, as a field, a CPU grid, an image or a Braille trace:\\n\\n| The product is about             | An ASCII effect drawn from it                                               |\\n| -------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| Maps, outdoors, travel           | Braille contour lines of real-looking terrain, a route over them            |\\n| Audio, music, voice              | A waveform or spectrum, lines stacked like a spectrogram                    |\\n| Money, trading, analytics        | A price line or volume ridges, a dense band where activity is               |\\n| Infrastructure, uptime, networks | Packets along paths, a grid of hosts, a pulse at the incident               |\\n| Weather, climate, energy         | Isobars, a radar sweep, heat rising off a surface                           |\\n| Sleep, health, calm              | A slow breathing ring, a tide line, a night sky                             |\\n| Food, drink, physical goods      | The product photo itself as ASCII (`sampleToField`), steam rising in glyphs |\\n| Space, science, research         | A night sky, orbits, a particle trace, a slow nebula                        |\\n| A developer tool or API          | The product\'s real output in a terminal pane: its CLI, its logs, its data   |\\n\\nA soft glow or drifting noise fits almost any product, which is why it says nothing about this one. Use one only as a supporting layer.\\n\\n## 3. Compose the page\\n\\nSpend the boldness in one place: the hero effect. Keep everything else quiet and disciplined.\\n\\n**Pick the hero that fits**, not the split layout by default:\\n\\n- **Full-bleed field.** The effect fills the hero and the headline sits on cleared ground (see [recipes.md](recipes.md#a-hero-that-clears-its-headline)).\\n- **The product shot.** A real product photo or screenshot converted to ASCII, large, with the headline beside or above it. It can resolve into the photo on hover.\\n- **One big number or object.** A rotating ASCII object or a Braille ring behind a real figure the product has, with the label in HTML.\\n- **A live demo.** A terminal pane that runs the product\'s real command, or an effect that responds to one control, so the page shows what the product does.\\n\\n**Build the rest from the content.** A section exists because the product has something to say there, not because landing pages have one.\\n\\n- Show, don\'t list. One wide section that shows a feature working beats three cards that name three features.\\n- Vary the rhythm: a wide section, then a narrow one; text left, then right. Avoid rows of identical cards.\\n- Use at most two or three ASCII effects on the page, each tied to content: the hero, a trace of real data, an ASCII product photo, one section break.\\n- One accent colour at most. The glyph ink carries the page\'s colour; buttons and text stay in the theme. A phosphor colour is an accent too.\\n- Typography carries the personality. One family is fine. Set a clear scale with weight and size, sentence case throughout.\\n- Keep vertical rhythm even (about `py-24` to `py-32` between sections on desktop, `py-16` on a phone) and line lengths under 80 characters.\\n\\n## 4. Write true, plain copy\\n\\n- The headline says what the product does for whom, in plain words. No wordplay that needs the subhead to explain it.\\n- The subhead adds two specifics: how it works, what you get.\\n- Buttons say what happens: \\"Download for Mac\\", \\"Start a free trial\\", \\"Read the docs\\".\\n- Use only facts from step 1. No invented stats, testimonials, customer logos, ratings or prices. If a section needs a number the product doesn\'t have, drop the section.\\n\\n## 5. Don\'t ship the tells\\n\\nThese make a page read as generated, whatever the effect looks like:\\n\\n- An all-caps, letter-spaced label above every heading, or monospace micro-labels used as decoration. ASCII invites terminal styling; use it where the product is a terminal, not as costume on every heading.\\n- Numbered markers (01, 02, 03) on things that aren\'t a sequence.\\n- One word in the headline set apart in italic, grey or colour; a serif display line followed by a grey second line.\\n- A row of three identical feature cards; a stats strip with invented numbers.\\n- Captions or pills laid over a visual (\\"Field study 01\\", \\"Signal / in motion\\"); a round arrow button on a card that goes nowhere.\\n- A fake product UI built from divs; fake testimonials; placeholder logos.\\n- A glowing orb or gradient in a rounded card as the whole hero.\\n- Em dashes, \\"elevate\\", \\"seamless\\", \\"unleash\\", \\"next-gen\\", an arrow on every link, decorative coloured dots.\\n\\n## 6. Look at it as a visitor\\n\\nScreenshot the whole page at 1440px and 390px, in light and dark mode. Go through every item in section 5 against the screenshots and the code, one by one, and fix each one you find; search the code for `—` and `uppercase`. Then answer honestly:\\n\\n- In five seconds, can you tell what the product is and what to do next?\\n- Is the ASCII effect the first thing you notice, and does it belong to this product?\\n- Would this page be mistaken for any other product\'s page? If yes, the hero or the copy is generic: go back to step 2.\\n- Remove one element that isn\'t earning its place.\\n\\nThen run every check in [quality.md](quality.md).\\n"},{"path":"skills/references/placement.md","language":"markdown","content":"# Putting an ASCII effect on a page\\n\\n## Layering\\n\\n`AsciiShader` and `AsciiGrid` fill their nearest positioned ancestor. Content goes in a sibling, above:\\n\\n```tsx\\n<section className=\\"relative isolate overflow-hidden\\">\\n  <HeroBackground className=\\"absolute inset-0 -z-10\\" />\\n  <div className=\\"relative mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-32\\">…</div>\\n</section>\\n```\\n\\n- `isolate` on the section keeps `-z-10` inside it, so the effect never slips behind the page background.\\n- The section needs height from its content or a `min-h-*`. A renderer takes the size it is given; zero height draws nothing.\\n- Round corners and borders go on the section with `overflow-hidden`, so the canvas is clipped to them.\\n- Renderers ignore the pointer unless `interactive` or `interaction` is set, so links and buttons above stay clickable. An interactive background still sits under content: give the content `pointer-events-none` on empty areas only if the effect should react there.\\n\\n## Landing pages\\n\\nRead [landing-pages.md](landing-pages.md) first. These are the placements.\\n\\n- **Hero:** a full-bleed effect behind the headline. Clear the area under the text (tone near 0) and let the effect peak toward the edges or a corner. Fade it out at the bottom with `smoothstep` on `uv.y` so the next section starts clean.\\n- **Clearing space for text:** measure the text block and pass its box to the field as a uniform. [recipes.md](recipes.md#a-hero-that-clears-its-headline) has the component. An ellipse in `uv` is too big on desktop and too small on a phone.\\n- **Section break:** a band 6 to 12 rows tall: a glyph gradient, a row of box-drawing characters, or a slow wave in Braille.\\n- **Feature card:** a small `AsciiGrid` or shader behind the top half of a card, under the icon and title.\\n- **Terminal pane:** a `rounded-lg border bg-black` box with an `AsciiGrid` or a `<pre>`, wrapped in `className=\\"dark\\"` so the tokens take their dark values.\\n- **Behind a big number or headline:** a glow or a rotating object centred behind the text. Real text stays HTML on top; never draw the headline into the field.\\n\\n## Meters, traces and status\\n\\n- **Traces and meters:** draw them in an `AsciiGrid` (Braille lines, `EIGHTHS_UP` bars, `blocks` for density) or as DOM text in a `<pre>`. Keep the value and the label as HTML text beside or over the glyphs.\\n- **Accessible names:** a trace canvas gets `role=\\"img\\"` and an `aria-label` with the data in words, or a visually hidden table. A meter gets `role=\\"meter\\"` with `aria-valuenow`, `aria-valuemin`, `aria-valuemax`.\\n- **Many on one page:** at most 8 `AsciiShader` instances hold a WebGL context at once; others show their fallback until one scrolls away. Use `AsciiGrid` (Canvas2D) or DOM text for grids of small pieces.\\n- **Colour meaning:** status colours from the palette (`\\"green\\"`, `\\"orange\\"`, `\\"red\\"`) map to good, warning and critical. Pair colour with a glyph or label so it never carries meaning alone (`█` up, `▒` degraded, `·` no data).\\n\\n## Sizing cells\\n\\n`--ascii-cell` sets the glyph height for the whole page (12px by default). Override it on a wrapper for one section, or pass `cell` to one component. 10 to 12px reads as texture, 14 to 18px as deliberate ASCII art, 20px and up as large letters. Cells always snap to whole device pixels; don\'t scale a renderer with CSS `transform: scale()`, which blurs the glyphs.\\n\\n## Next.js and server components\\n\\n- Files that use `AsciiShader`, `AsciiGrid`, `useAsciiColors`, `useFrameLoop` or a canvas start with `\\"use client\\"`. A server page imports and renders them like any component.\\n- `AsciiPre`, `frameToText`, `mapTones` and the charset helpers run on the server too.\\n- Webcam and file input stay in the browser: read them with `useMediaElement` in a client component and never upload them.\\n"},{"path":"skills/references/quality.md","language":"markdown","content":"# Quality checks\\n\\nRun every check before calling an effect done. Each is something you can see or measure.\\n\\n## Glyphs\\n\\n- Individual glyphs read at 100% zoom. Zoom into a screenshot: you see characters, not a blur, not pixel dots, not a flat block.\\n- Glyphs are crisp at device pixel ratio 1, 2 and 3, and after browser zoom. A blurry result means the canvas is scaled by CSS or the cell isn\'t whole device pixels; let the renderer size itself.\\n- The effect uses more than two or three glyphs. A field stuck between two tones draws a flat pattern; widen its range or raise `grade.contrast`.\\n- Shapes are round where they should be (aspect corrected with `res.x / res.y`) and look right in both `standard` and `braille`.\\n- The box has the intended size at 1440px and 390px wide, with no horizontal scroll at 390px.\\n- Light and dark mode both look intentional. Toggle the `.dark` class to check; the media query alone doesn\'t change a shadcn theme.\\n\\n## Text and contrast\\n\\n- Text over glyphs has 4.5:1 contrast against the densest glyphs behind it, or sits on cleared ground or its own panel.\\n- Real text stays real: headings, labels and values are HTML, selectable and read by screen readers. Never draw copy into a canvas.\\n\\n## Motion and accessibility\\n\\n- With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, the renderer root has `data-state=\\"static\\"` and shows a finished frame (`stillTime`). Emulate it: `agent-browser set media dark reduced-motion`, Playwright `page.emulateMedia({ reducedMotion: \\"reduce\\" })`, or DevTools Rendering.\\n- Offscreen and in a hidden tab, the root reports `data-state=\\"paused\\"`.\\n- Decorative canvases are `aria-hidden` (the renderers set it by default). Data visuals have an accessible name with the value.\\n- Nothing flashes more than three times a second. Glyphs that change every frame at random (`hash21` with raw `t`) flicker; quantise time.\\n- Controls you add (charset pickers, export buttons) are keyboard reachable with a visible focus ring.\\n\\n## Performance\\n\\n- At most 8 `AsciiShader`s are live at a time. Use `AsciiGrid` or DOM text for many small pieces.\\n- The `field` string is a module-level constant; changing values go through `uniforms`.\\n- Keep fields cheap: `fbm` is four noise calls, and Braille samples eight points per glyph. A field calling `fbm` a dozen times can drop frames on a phone at small cell sizes.\\n- `AsciiGrid` draw functions allocate nothing per frame: create tone fields and arrays once per size and reuse them.\\n- Images and video are sampled at grid size, never at full camera resolution.\\n\\n## Console\\n\\n- No compile errors, `uniform … skipped` warnings, or WebGL context-lost messages.\\n- No hydration warnings: client-only values (theme colours, sizes, `Math.random`) are read in effects or hooks, not during render.\\n\\n## Checking state in a browser\\n\\n```js\\n[...document.querySelectorAll(\\"[data-state]\\")].map((el) => el.dataset.state);\\n// \\"running\\" on screen, \\"static\\" under reduced motion, \\"paused\\" offscreen, \\"fallback\\" when nothing drew\\n```\\n"},{"path":"skills/references/recipes.md","language":"markdown","content":"# Recipes\\n\\nComplete components to adapt. Each one is a starting point: change the field, the charset and the props to fit what the user asked for. Imports use `@/lib/ascii`; use the project\'s alias.\\n\\n## A shader background\\n\\nA slow, theme-coloured field with the engine\'s look props passed through, so the user (or a Studio) can switch charset and colours, and a `handle` for export.\\n\\n```tsx\\n\\"use client\\";\\n\\nimport type { AsciiSceneProps } from \\"@/lib/ascii\\";\\nimport { AsciiShader } from \\"@/lib/ascii/shader\\";\\n\\nconst FIELD = /* glsl */ `\\nfloat field(vec2 uv, vec2 res, float t) {\\n  vec2 p = vec2(uv.x * res.x / res.y, uv.y);\\n  float drift = fbm(p * 1.6 + vec2(t * 0.03, t * 0.01));\\n  float bands = 0.5 + 0.5 * sin((p.y + drift * 0.8) * 9.0 - t * 0.2);\\n  float tone = smoothstep(0.15, 0.95, bands * drift * 1.6) * smoothstep(0.0, 0.25, uv.y);\\n  return tone * tideIntensity;\\n}`;\\n\\nexport interface TideBackgroundProps extends AsciiSceneProps {\\n  /** How strong the pattern is, 0 to 1. Default 0.9. */\\n  intensity?: number;\\n}\\n\\nexport const TideBackground = ({\\n  intensity = 0.9,\\n  charset = \\"braille\\",\\n  colorMode = \\"mono\\",\\n  stillTime = 6,\\n  ...props\\n}: TideBackgroundProps) => (\\n  <AsciiShader\\n    field={FIELD}\\n    uniforms={{ tideIntensity: intensity }}\\n    charset={charset}\\n    colorMode={colorMode}\\n    stillTime={stillTime}\\n    {...props}\\n  />\\n);\\n```\\n\\nEvery uniform you pass must be read in the field, or the compiler drops it and the engine warns.\\n\\n## A hero that clears its headline\\n\\nMeasure the text block and clear a rounded box around it, so the headline sits on clean ground at every width.\\n\\n```tsx\\n\\"use client\\";\\n\\nimport { useEffect, useRef, useState } from \\"react\\";\\n\\nimport { AsciiShader } from \\"@/lib/ascii/shader\\";\\n\\nconst FIELD = /* glsl */ `\\nfloat heroBox(vec2 p, vec4 box, float radius) {\\n  vec2 centre = box.xy + box.zw * 0.5;\\n  vec2 d = abs(p - centre) - box.zw * 0.5 + radius;\\n  return length(max(d, 0.0)) + min(max(d.x, d.y), 0.0) - radius;\\n}\\n\\nfloat field(vec2 uv, vec2 res, float t) {\\n  float aspect = res.x / res.y;\\n  vec2 p = vec2(uv.x * aspect, uv.y);\\n  float rings = 0.5 + 0.5 * sin(length(p - vec2(aspect * 0.8, 0.2)) * 28.0 - t * 0.6);\\n  float glyphs = smoothstep(0.35, 1.0, rings) * (0.4 + 0.6 * fbm(p * 2.0 + t * 0.02));\\n  // heroText is the text block in the same square units, y up.\\n  vec4 box = vec4(heroText.x * aspect, heroText.y, heroText.z * aspect, heroText.w);\\n  float clear = smoothstep(0.0, 0.08, heroBox(p, box, 0.04));\\n  return glyphs * clear;\\n}`;\\n\\nexport const Hero = () => {\\n  const section = useRef<HTMLElement>(null);\\n  const text = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\\n  const [box, setBox] = useState<[number, number, number, number]>([\\n    0, 0, 0, 0,\\n  ]);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const measure = () => {\\n      const outer = section.current?.getBoundingClientRect();\\n      const inner = text.current?.getBoundingClientRect();\\n      if (!(outer && inner && outer.width && outer.height)) {\\n        return;\\n      }\\n      // uv space: x and width over the section width, y up from the bottom.\\n      setBox([\\n        (inner.left - outer.left) / outer.width,\\n        (outer.bottom - inner.bottom) / outer.height,\\n        inner.width / outer.width,\\n        inner.height / outer.height,\\n      ]);\\n    };\\n    measure();\\n    const observer = new ResizeObserver(measure);\\n    if (section.current) {\\n      observer.observe(section.current);\\n    }\\n    return () => observer.disconnect();\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <section ref={section} className=\\"relative isolate overflow-hidden\\">\\n      <AsciiShader\\n        field={FIELD}\\n        uniforms={{ heroText: box }}\\n        charset=\\"standard\\"\\n        className=\\"absolute inset-0 -z-10\\"\\n      />\\n      <div\\n        ref={text}\\n        className=\\"relative mx-auto max-w-2xl px-6 py-32 text-center\\"\\n      >\\n        <h1 className=\\"text-5xl font-semibold tracking-tight\\">\\n          Ship logs you can read\\n        </h1>\\n        <p className=\\"text-muted-foreground mt-4 text-lg\\">\\n          One sentence about the product.\\n        </p>\\n      </div>\\n    </section>\\n  );\\n};\\n```\\n\\n## A spinning object on the CPU\\n\\nA torus shaded with a light, drawn into an `AsciiGrid` through `mapTones`. The same pattern draws any 3D mesh, heightmap or simulation: compute a tone per sample, then map.\\n\\n```tsx\\n\\"use client\\";\\n\\nimport { useRef } from \\"react\\";\\n\\nimport {\\n  AsciiGrid,\\n  createToneField,\\n  mapTones,\\n  resolveCharset,\\n} from \\"@/lib/ascii\\";\\nimport type { AsciiGridDrawContext, CharsetName, ToneField } from \\"@/lib/ascii\\";\\n\\nexport interface SpinningTorusProps {\\n  /** Glyph set. Default `\\"standard\\"`. */\\n  charset?: CharsetName;\\n  /** Cell height in CSS px. Default `--ascii-cell`. */\\n  cell?: number;\\n  className?: string;\\n}\\n\\nexport const SpinningTorus = ({\\n  charset = \\"standard\\",\\n  cell,\\n  className,\\n}: SpinningTorusProps) => {\\n  const fieldRef = useRef<ToneField | null>(null);\\n  const depthRef = useRef(new Float32Array(0));\\n\\n  const draw = ({\\n    frame,\\n    columns,\\n    rows,\\n    time,\\n    metrics,\\n  }: AsciiGridDrawContext) => {\\n    const set = resolveCharset(charset);\\n    const [gx, gy] = set.grid;\\n    let field = fieldRef.current;\\n    if (!field || field.width !== columns * gx || field.height !== rows * gy) {\\n      field = createToneField(columns, rows, set);\\n      fieldRef.current = field;\\n      depthRef.current = new Float32Array(field.tones.length);\\n    }\\n    field.tones.fill(0);\\n    const depth = depthRef.current;\\n    depth.fill(0);\\n    const a = time * 0.7;\\n    const b = time * 0.4;\\n    // Sample aspect: cells are metrics.width wide and metrics.height tall.\\n    const aspect =\\n      (field.width * metrics.width) /\\n      gx /\\n      ((field.height * metrics.height) / gy);\\n    for (let theta = 0; theta < 6.283; theta += 0.04) {\\n      for (let phi = 0; phi < 6.283; phi += 0.015) {\\n        const circle = 2 + Math.cos(theta);\\n        const x0 = circle * Math.cos(phi);\\n        const y0 = Math.sin(theta);\\n        const z0 = circle * Math.sin(phi);\\n        const y = y0 * Math.cos(a) - z0 * Math.sin(a);\\n        const z1 = y0 * Math.sin(a) + z0 * Math.cos(a);\\n        const x = x0 * Math.cos(b) - z1 * Math.sin(b);\\n        const z = 6 + x0 * Math.sin(b) + z1 * Math.cos(b);\\n        const inv = 1 / z;\\n        const u = 0.5 + (x * inv * 1.4) / aspect;\\n        const v = 0.5 - y * inv * 1.4;\\n        const sx = Math.floor(u * field.width);\\n        const sy = Math.floor(v * field.height);\\n        if (sx < 0 || sy < 0 || sx >= field.width || sy >= field.height) {\\n          continue;\\n        }\\n        const i = sy * field.width + sx;\\n        if (inv > (depth[i] ?? 0)) {\\n          depth[i] = inv;\\n          const nx = Math.cos(theta) * Math.cos(phi);\\n          const ny = Math.sin(theta);\\n          const light = Math.max(0, nx * 0.5 + ny * 0.7 + 0.3);\\n          field.tones[i] = 0.15 + 0.85 * light;\\n        }\\n      }\\n    }\\n    mapTones(field, columns, rows, { charset: set }, frame);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <AsciiGrid draw={draw} cell={cell} stillTime={1.2} className={className} />\\n  );\\n};\\n```\\n\\n## A Braille trace\\n\\nA line through a list of values: a waveform, a CPU trace in a terminal pane, the height profile of a route. Two dots across and four down per glyph make the line four times sharper than one glyph per point. Draw it with `brailleChar`, and keep the numbers in HTML.\\n\\n```tsx\\n\\"use client\\";\\n\\nimport { AsciiGrid, BRAILLE_DOT_BITS, brailleChar } from \\"@/lib/ascii\\";\\nimport type { AsciiGridDrawContext } from \\"@/lib/ascii\\";\\n\\nexport interface BrailleTraceProps {\\n  /** Values in any range; the trace scales to min and max. */\\n  data: readonly number[];\\n  /** Text equivalent for screen readers, such as \\"Requests per minute, last hour: 120 to 340\\". */\\n  label: string;\\n  className?: string;\\n}\\n\\nexport const BrailleTrace = ({ data, label, className }: BrailleTraceProps) => {\\n  const draw = ({ frame, columns, rows }: AsciiGridDrawContext) => {\\n    const width = columns * 2;\\n    const height = rows * 4;\\n    const masks = new Uint8Array(columns * rows);\\n    const min = Math.min(...data);\\n    const span = Math.max(...data) - min || 1;\\n    const yAt = (x: number) => {\\n      const t = (x / (width - 1)) * (data.length - 1);\\n      const i = Math.floor(t);\\n      const value =\\n        (data[i] ?? 0) +\\n        ((data[i + 1] ?? data[i] ?? 0) - (data[i] ?? 0)) * (t - i);\\n      return Math.round((1 - (value - min) / span) * (height - 1));\\n    };\\n    for (let x = 0; x < width; x += 1) {\\n      const y0 = yAt(x);\\n      const y1 = x + 1 < width ? yAt(x + 1) : y0;\\n      // Fill the vertical gap so steep segments stay connected.\\n      for (let y = Math.min(y0, y1); y <= Math.max(y0, y1); y += 1) {\\n        const cell = Math.floor(y / 4) * columns + Math.floor(x / 2);\\n        masks[cell] =\\n          (masks[cell] ?? 0) | (BRAILLE_DOT_BITS[y % 4]?.[x % 2] ?? 0);\\n      }\\n    }\\n    masks.forEach((mask, i) => {\\n      if (mask) {\\n        frame.put(i % columns, Math.floor(i / columns), brailleChar(mask));\\n      }\\n    });\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <AsciiGrid\\n      draw={draw}\\n      animate={false}\\n      role=\\"img\\"\\n      aria-hidden={false}\\n      aria-label={label}\\n      className={className}\\n    />\\n  );\\n};\\n```\\n\\n## A bar meter\\n\\nEighth blocks give a progress bar eight steps per cell. This one is plain DOM text, so it renders on the server and reads in a screen reader.\\n\\n```tsx\\nimport { eighthsBar } from \\"@/lib/ascii\\";\\n\\nexport const UsageMeter = ({\\n  value,\\n  label,\\n}: {\\n  value: number;\\n  label: string;\\n}) => (\\n  <div className=\\"font-mono text-sm\\">\\n    <div className=\\"flex justify-between\\">\\n      <span>{label}</span>\\n      <span>{Math.round(value * 100)}%</span>\\n    </div>\\n    <div\\n      role=\\"meter\\"\\n      aria-label={label}\\n      aria-valuemin={0}\\n      aria-valuemax={100}\\n      aria-valuenow={Math.round(value * 100)}\\n      className=\\"bg-muted/40 text-ascii-ink whitespace-pre leading-none\\"\\n    >\\n      {eighthsBar(value, 32)}\\n    </div>\\n  </div>\\n);\\n```\\n\\n## A terminal pane\\n\\nBox-drawing frame, a typed line and a blinking cursor, in a phosphor colour. Wrap it in `dark` so it stays a dark screen on a light page.\\n\\n```tsx\\n\\"use client\\";\\n\\nimport { AsciiGrid, BOX, PHOSPHORS } from \\"@/lib/ascii\\";\\nimport type { AsciiGridDrawContext } from \\"@/lib/ascii\\";\\n\\nconst LINES = [\\n  \\"$ deploy --prod\\",\\n  \\"building...      done in 4.2s\\",\\n  \\"uploading...     12 files\\",\\n  \\"live at https://example.com\\",\\n];\\n\\nconst draw = ({ frame, columns, rows, time }: AsciiGridDrawContext) => {\\n  const box = BOX.rounded;\\n  frame.write(0, 0, box.tl + box.h.repeat(columns - 2) + box.tr);\\n  for (let y = 1; y < rows - 1; y += 1) {\\n    frame.put(0, y, box.v);\\n    frame.put(columns - 1, y, box.v);\\n  }\\n  frame.write(0, rows - 1, box.bl + box.h.repeat(columns - 2) + box.br);\\n  // Type one character every 40ms, line by line.\\n  let budget = Math.floor(time * 25);\\n  LINES.forEach((line, i) => {\\n    const shown = line.slice(0, Math.max(0, budget));\\n    budget -= line.length;\\n    frame.write(2, 1 + i, shown);\\n    if (budget < 0 && budget > -line.length - 1 && time % 1 < 0.5) {\\n      frame.put(2 + shown.length, 1 + i, \\"█\\");\\n    }\\n  });\\n};\\n\\nexport const DeployTerminal = () => (\\n  <div className=\\"dark relative h-40 rounded-lg bg-neutral-950\\">\\n    <AsciiGrid\\n      draw={draw}\\n      ink={PHOSPHORS.p1.ink}\\n      stillTime={10}\\n      className=\\"absolute inset-0\\"\\n    />\\n  </div>\\n);\\n```\\n\\nEach phosphor in `PHOSPHORS` has an `ink` and the near-black `paper` of its glass; pass `paper={PHOSPHORS.p1.paper}` to paint the glass instead of the wrapper.\\n\\n## An image as ASCII\\n\\nSample an image into a tone field each frame (or once, for a still), then map it. The same code takes a `<video>` or a webcam `MediaStream`.\\n\\n```tsx\\n\\"use client\\";\\n\\nimport { useRef } from \\"react\\";\\n\\nimport {\\n  AsciiGrid,\\n  autoLevels,\\n  mapTones,\\n  sampleToField,\\n  useMediaElement,\\n} from \\"@/lib/ascii\\";\\nimport type {\\n  AsciiGridDrawContext,\\n  CharsetName,\\n  ColorMode,\\n  ToneField,\\n} from \\"@/lib/ascii\\";\\n\\nexport interface AsciiPhotoProps {\\n  src: string;\\n  /** What the photo shows, for screen readers. */\\n  alt: string;\\n  /** Default `\\"standard\\"`. */\\n  charset?: CharsetName;\\n  /** Default `\\"truecolor\\"`. */\\n  colorMode?: ColorMode;\\n  className?: string;\\n}\\n\\nexport const AsciiPhoto = ({\\n  src,\\n  alt,\\n  charset = \\"standard\\",\\n  colorMode = \\"truecolor\\",\\n  className,\\n}: AsciiPhotoProps) => {\\n  const { element } = useMediaElement(src);\\n  const fieldRef = useRef<ToneField | undefined>(undefined);\\n\\n  const draw = ({ frame, columns, rows, metrics }: AsciiGridDrawContext) => {\\n    if (!element) {\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    const field = sampleToField(element, {\\n      aspect: (columns * metrics.width) / (rows * metrics.height),\\n      charset,\\n      columns,\\n      fit: \\"cover\\",\\n      into: fieldRef.current,\\n      rows,\\n    });\\n    fieldRef.current = field;\\n    autoLevels(field);\\n    mapTones(\\n      field,\\n      columns,\\n      rows,\\n      { charset, colorMode, grade: { contrast: 1.2 } },\\n      frame\\n    );\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <AsciiGrid\\n      draw={draw}\\n      colored={colorMode !== \\"mono\\"}\\n      animate={false}\\n      role=\\"img\\"\\n      aria-hidden={false}\\n      aria-label={alt}\\n      className={className}\\n    />\\n  );\\n};\\n```\\n\\n`useMediaElement(source)` takes a URL, a `Blob` (a file the user picked) or a `MediaStream`, and returns `{ element, kind, error }`; show a message when `error` is set. `into` reuses the field between frames, and `aspect` tells the sampler the box\'s real shape so the image isn\'t stretched. For video, leave `animate` on so each frame resamples; for a webcam, pass the stream from `navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia({ video: true })`, set `mirror: true`, and stop its tracks on unmount. Frames never leave the browser.\\n\\n## A full background with colour, CRT and the pointer\\n\\nCombine a `field`, a `colorField`, CRT effects and pointer parallax into a full animated piece. For a background that is a picture of a place (a landscape, a city), follow [scenes.md](scenes.md) instead: the city and landscape backgrounds are painted halftone scenes with calm motion and no pointer response.\\n\\n```tsx\\n\\"use client\\";\\n\\nimport { CRT_FX } from \\"@/lib/ascii\\";\\nimport type { AsciiSceneProps } from \\"@/lib/ascii\\";\\nimport { AsciiShader } from \\"@/lib/ascii/shader\\";\\n\\nconst SHARED = /* glsl */ `\\nfloat duneHeight(vec2 p, float t) {\\n  return 0.35 + 0.12 * snoise(vec2(p.x * 1.4 + t * 0.05, 3.0)) + 0.05 * snoise(vec2(p.x * 5.0, t * 0.1));\\n}`;\\n\\nconst FIELD = /* glsl */ `${SHARED}\\nfloat field(vec2 uv, vec2 res, float t) {\\n  vec2 p = vec2(uv.x * res.x / res.y, uv.y);\\n  p.x += (u_pointer.x - 0.5) * 0.15 * u_pointer.z;     // parallax\\n  float h = duneHeight(p, t);\\n  float ground = smoothstep(h + 1.5 / res.y, h, p.y) * (0.45 + 0.5 * (h - p.y + 0.3));\\n  vec2 sun = vec2(res.x / res.y * 0.7, 0.62);\\n  float glow = exp(-dot(p - sun, p - sun) * 30.0);\\n  return max(ground, glow);\\n}`;\\n\\nconst COLOR_FIELD = /* glsl */ `${SHARED}\\nvec3 colorField(vec2 uv, vec2 res, float t) {\\n  vec2 p = vec2(uv.x * res.x / res.y, uv.y);\\n  float sky = smoothstep(0.2, 1.0, uv.y);\\n  vec3 sand = toSrgb(oklch(0.62, 0.11, 0.9));\\n  vec3 dusk = toSrgb(oklch(0.55, 0.16, 5.2));\\n  return p.y < duneHeight(p, t) ? sand : mix(sand, dusk, sky);\\n}`;\\n\\nexport const DuneBackground = ({\\n  charset = \\"dense\\",\\n  fx = CRT_FX,\\n  stillTime = 4,\\n  ...props\\n}: AsciiSceneProps) => (\\n  <AsciiShader\\n    field={FIELD}\\n    colorField={COLOR_FIELD}\\n    charset={charset}\\n    fx={fx}\\n    stillTime={stillTime}\\n    interaction=\\"none\\"\\n    {...props}\\n  />\\n);\\n```\\n\\n`SHARED` appears word for word in both strings, so the engine compiles it once.\\n"},{"path":"skills/references/scenes.md","language":"markdown","content":"# Painted scenes\\n\\nA painted scene is a full-bleed background that reads as a picture: a place, a time of day and one light, drawn as a halftone of coloured dots. Every cell is painted, including the darkest sky. This is how the landscape and city backgrounds on {{SITE_URL}}/backgrounds are built. Use this file when the user asks for a background, a hero scene, a landscape, a cityscape or \\"a picture of X in ASCII\\" that should fill a section.\\n\\nYou build the scene; you don\'t pick one from the catalogue. The shipped backgrounds (`glacier-lagoon`, `balloon-valley`, `san-francisco`, `lighthouse` and the rest) are examples to borrow technique from. Install one only when it is exactly what the user asked for.\\n\\n## The look\\n\\n| Prop        | Value                   | Why                                                                                                   |\\n| ----------- | ----------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| `charset`   | `\\"dots\\"`                | ` ·∙•⦁●`: five dot sizes after the empty cell. The two smallest are specks, for quiet sky and shadow  |\\n| `square`    | `true`                  | Cells as wide as tall, so dots lie on an even lattice. Without it, dots form rows and read as stripes |\\n| `cell`      | `7`                     | About 200 columns across 1440px. Shapes stay big; detail doesn\'t alias into noise                     |\\n| `colorMode` | `\\"truecolor\\"`           | Every dot gets its own colour from your `colorField`                                                  |\\n| `dither`    | `\\"bayer4\\"`              | Tones between two dot sizes mix them in a fixed pattern, so gradients don\'t band                      |\\n| `paper`     | the scene\'s darkest sky | The scene paints its own ground, so it looks the same in light and dark mode                          |\\n\\n`square` changes the cell, not the field contract: `res.x / res.y` is still the box\'s true aspect, `dots` is one sample per cell, and `floor(uv * res)` is one cell. Braille and quadrants stretch on square cells, so keep `square` for round-glyph ramps.\\n\\n## Step by step\\n\\n### 1. Write the brief in one sentence\\n\\nPlace, time, one light, and what sits where: \\"A lake at night between snowy peaks, a full moon right of centre, its path on the water, a dark near shore bottom left.\\" One focal light only. Pick a short palette for it (night blue, violet haze, moon cream; or cream paper, slate blue, gold) and write the colours as `vec3` constants. Fewer hues read as one picture.\\n\\n### 2. Set up coordinates for every screen shape\\n\\n```glsl\\nfloat aspect = res.x / res.y;\\nfloat squeeze = sqrt(clamp(1.6 / aspect, 1.0, 4.0));    // narrow boxes see more width\\nfloat portrait = 1.0 - smoothstep(0.7, 1.1, aspect);    // 0 on desktop, 1 on a phone\\nfloat halfW = 0.5 * aspect * squeeze;                   // x runs -halfW..halfW\\nvec2 p = vec2((uv.x - 0.5) * aspect * squeeze, uv.y);   // y 0..1, up\\n```\\n\\n- Place features at a share of the half width (`0.55 * halfW`), not at a fixed x, so the composition holds from a phone to an ultrawide.\\n- Anything round (a sun, a moon, a halo, a light path) is measured with the squeeze undone: `length(vec2((p.x - c.x) / squeeze, p.y - c.y))`. Otherwise it turns oval on a phone.\\n- Use `portrait` to reframe for phones, not just squeeze. A hero headline sits in the middle of the screen and its buttons just below, about `uv.y` 0.38 to 0.65 on a 390x844 phone. Move the horizon and the subject below the buttons, or the light into the empty sky above the headline. On desktop keep the focal light out of the middle half of the width.\\n\\n### 3. Paint far to near\\n\\nWrite one function that returns the colour of a point, painting layers back to front, each nearer layer over the last: sky, far range, middle layer, near layer, water, foreground, then mist. Give each layer a depth so lighting and haze know where it is.\\n\\nKeep a value ladder:\\n\\n- The sky is brightest near the light and the horizon and darkest overhead.\\n- The subject carries the brightest values after the light: lit snow, lit facades, lit ice. Dark silhouettes against a bright sky draw nothing but specks; the scene goes black.\\n- Each nearer layer is darker and more saturated in shadow; each farther layer is lighter, cooler and closer to the sky colour. Mix far layers toward the horizon sky colour (`mix(face, haze, 0.3)` far, `0.0` near), and every layer\'s foot into haze too.\\n- One foreground plane (a shore, a rail, a rooftop) near the frame edge gives scale. Keep it dark with a lit rim.\\n- In numbers: colours under about 0.15 value draw specks, 0.2 to 0.6 draw the middle dots that carry the picture, and above 0.8 draw the largest. Most of the frame should sit between 0.2 and 0.6.\\n- Water repeats the sky at 60 to 90 percent of its value. A black sky makes black water, and half the frame goes dead. A night scene still needs a lit sky: a cloud deck lit by the moon or the town, a glow along the horizon.\\n- Check the values before the colours: render once with `colorMode=\\"mono\\"`, which sizes the dots by tone alone. If the shapes don\'t read in mono, colour won\'t save them.\\n\\n### 4. Shape natural forms with light, not outlines\\n\\n- Silhouettes come from noise at two scales: `fbm(x * f)` for the mass, `1.0 - abs(snoise(...))` for sharper peaks. Nothing in nature is a straight edge, an even repeat or a stripe.\\n- Shade a face by the silhouette\'s normal against the light: `normal = normalize(vec2(-slope, 1.0))`, `lit = smoothstep(0.1, 0.8, dot(normal, toLight))`. Measure the slope over a window that widens below the crest and at an x that wanders with noise, or lit and shadow faces split on vertical lines.\\n- Break faces with ribs down the fall line (noise stretched along y), a snow line or strata, and a rim of light along crests that face the light.\\n- More detail near, less far. Far layers are soft shapes in haze.\\n- Size things in cells. At `cell` 7 a 900px-tall box has about 128 rows, so one row is about 0.008 of `uv.y`. A shape needs 8 or more cells across to read as a shape; a point of light needs 1 to 3. Draw a town as a few big houses with lit windows, not a grid of tiny ones.\\n- Built things (houses, quays, boats) can have straight edges, but vary them: stagger rows, leave gaps, vary heights and roofs, and switch windows on per window with a hash.\\n\\n### 5. Light, reflections and air\\n\\n- The light is a disc, a tight halo (`exp(-r * r * 260.0)`) and a wide glow (`exp(-r * 3.0)`). Clouds near it pick up its colour; clouds away from it stay dark.\\n- Water mirrors the scene: call your above-water function at `vec2(p.x + wobble, 2.0 * horizon - p.y)`, darken and cool it, and grow the ripple wobble toward the viewer. A light path is a column of glints under the light that widens toward the viewer.\\n- A lamp or a window lights what is near it. Add `lampColour * exp(-r * 9.0)` to the walls, quays and hulls around it, not only a glow in the air.\\n- A dark object on dark ground vanishes. Put it in front of light (a reflection, a glowing sky, a lamp\'s streak on the water) or give it a lit edge.\\n- Mist is a thin band of `fbm` along the horizon or a valley floor, drifting slowly.\\n\\n### 6. Turn colour into dots\\n\\n`field` and `colorField` compile into one shader and run for the same sample, one after the other. Paint once in a shared function, cache the result, and return the tone from `field` and the colour from `colorField`:\\n\\n- Tone comes from the colour\'s value, so dot size and colour always agree.\\n- Quiet darks: below a low value, push the colour toward a shade just above the paper and shrink the tone, so dark sky and shadow read as air (specks, some empty cells), not a field of visible dots.\\n- Lift dark ink colours (`col / peak * pow(peak, 0.6)`): small dots read darker than the colour they carry.\\n- Pin the focal light to a full dot (`tone = max(tone, smoothstep(0.82, 0.92, value))`), or it dithers between the two largest dots.\\n- An `intensity` uniform (0 to 1) dims the whole scene and quiets an ellipse in the middle most, for a headline.\\n\\nOn a light paper (a cream dawn, a white sky), take the tone from the distance to the paper colour instead, so near-paper sky is the faintest dots:\\n\\n```glsl\\nfloat inkAmount = length((col - PAPER) * vec3(1.0, 1.15, 0.85));\\nfloat tone = clamp(0.05 + 0.88 * pow(min(inkAmount / 0.8, 1.0), 1.1), 0.0, 1.0);\\n```\\n\\n### 7. Motion\\n\\nSlow and sparse: drifting cloud and mist (0.004 to 0.015 units a second), ripples and glints on water, a faint star twinkle with `sin`, a slow glow pulse (`1.0 + 0.05 * sin(t * 0.3)`), at most one or two slow movers. No rain, beams, searchlights, lightning, fireworks or pointer reactions. Never `hash` anything with raw `t` in it: it flickers. Pick `stillTime` so the reduced-motion frame looks finished.\\n\\n### 8. Wrap it in a component\\n\\nProps: `intensity` (default 1), the look props passed through with the defaults above, `square` (default `true`), `paper` (default: the scene\'s own in `truecolor`, `ansi16` and `duotone`; the theme\'s otherwise), a `fallback` gradient in the scene\'s colours, and `stillTime`. Keep `field` a module-level constant; anything the user tweaks goes through `uniforms`.\\n\\n## Starter scene\\n\\nA complete, working scene: a moonlit lake between snowy peaks. Copy it, rename the `lake` prefix to your own everywhere (including the `lakeIntensity` key in `uniforms`, which must match the GLSL name), then replace the sky, the layers and the water with your subject. It follows every step above.\\n\\nWrite the GLSL by [the rules in fields.md](fields.md#the-rules-of-the-language): float literals with a decimal point, constant loop bounds, and no reserved words as names. Short local names such as `flat`, `input`, `output`, `half` or `fixed` don\'t compile; the console names the line.\\n\\n```tsx\\n\\"use client\\";\\n\\nimport type { AsciiColor, AsciiSceneProps, ColorMode } from \\"@/lib/ascii\\";\\nimport { AsciiShader } from \\"@/lib/ascii/shader\\";\\n\\n// A moonlit lake between hills: the starter scene from the painted scenes\\n// guide. The scene is painted in colour first; the tone (dot size) is read\\n// back from that colour, so size and colour always agree.\\nconst SCENE = `\\n// Set once per sample by lakeColor and read by every layer.\\nfloat lakeT;\\nfloat lakeHorizon;\\nfloat lakeHalfW;\\nfloat lakeScale;\\nfloat lakeSqueeze;\\nvec2 lakeMoon;\\n\\n// A stable random value per cell. hash21 repeats every 50 whole cells.\\nfloat lakeHash(vec2 p) {\\n  return fract(sin(dot(p, vec2(12.9898, 78.233))) * 43758.5453);\\n}\\n\\n// Deep blue overhead, dusty violet at the horizon, a cool glow around the\\n// moon, thin cloud lit only near it, and sparse stars high up.\\nvec3 lakeSky(vec2 p, vec2 cellId) {\\n  float h = clamp((p.y - lakeHorizon) / (1.0 - lakeHorizon), 0.0, 1.0);\\n  vec3 col = mix(vec3(0.44, 0.38, 0.58), vec3(0.13, 0.14, 0.3), smoothstep(0.0, 0.35, h));\\n  col = mix(col, vec3(0.04, 0.05, 0.12), smoothstep(0.3, 1.0, h));\\n  // Undo the phone squeeze so the moon and its halo stay round.\\n  float r = length(vec2((p.x - lakeMoon.x) / lakeSqueeze, p.y - lakeMoon.y));\\n  col += vec3(0.2, 0.21, 0.3) * exp(-r * 3.0);\\n  col += vec3(0.5, 0.5, 0.56) * exp(-r * r * 260.0);\\n  float streak = fbm(vec2(p.x * 1.1 - lakeT * 0.006, p.y * 9.0));\\n  float cloud = smoothstep(0.55, 0.75, streak) * smoothstep(0.04, 0.16, h) * (1.0 - smoothstep(0.45, 0.65, h));\\n  vec3 cloudCol = mix(vec3(0.1, 0.1, 0.2), vec3(0.62, 0.6, 0.7), exp(-r * 5.0));\\n  col = mix(col, cloudCol, cloud * 0.7);\\n  float star = step(0.994, lakeHash(cellId)) * smoothstep(0.35, 0.7, h);\\n  col += vec3(0.6, 0.62, 0.7) * star * (0.6 + 0.4 * sin(lakeT * 0.7 + lakeHash(cellId + 3.0) * 40.0));\\n  float disc = 1.0 - smoothstep(0.034, 0.04, r);\\n  return mix(col, vec3(1.0, 0.96, 0.86), disc);\\n}\\n\\n// The top of layer i (0 the far snow range, 1 wooded hills, 2 the near\\n// shore) above the horizon. Two noise scales, so no edge is straight and\\n// no two peaks match.\\nfloat lakeRidge(float x, float i) {\\n  float freq = 1.1 + i * 0.6;\\n  float n = fbm(vec2(x * freq + i * 7.3, i * 3.1));\\n  float ridged = 1.0 - abs(snoise(vec2(x * freq * 0.8, i + 9.0)));\\n  float height = mix(0.26, 0.07, i * 0.5) * mix(n, ridged, mix(0.6, 0.2, i * 0.5)) * lakeScale;\\n  // The near layer dips under the water in the middle and right, so it\\n  // frames the lake instead of crossing it.\\n  float near = step(1.5, i) * smoothstep(-0.1, 0.5, x / lakeHalfW);\\n  return lakeHorizon + mix(0.01, -0.03, i * 0.5) + height - near * 0.12;\\n}\\n\\nvec3 lakeAbove(vec2 p, vec2 cellId) {\\n  vec3 col = lakeSky(p, cellId);\\n  vec3 haze = lakeSky(vec2(p.x, lakeHorizon + 0.03), cellId) * 0.8;\\n  // Far to near: each nearer layer paints over the ones behind it.\\n  for (int k = 0; k < 3; k++) {\\n    float i = float(k);\\n    float top = lakeRidge(p.x, i);\\n    if (p.y < top) {\\n      // Shade by the silhouette\'s normal against the moon. Below the crest\\n      // the slope is read over a wider window at a wandering x, so faces\\n      // spread into broad planes split by crooked ridges, not vertical\\n      // lines; ribs down the fall line break them up.\\n      float below = top - p.y;\\n      float xs = p.x + below * 0.5 * snoise(vec2(below * 5.0, p.x * 3.0 + i));\\n      float e = 0.01 + below * 0.8;\\n      float slope = (lakeRidge(xs + e, i) - lakeRidge(xs - e, i)) / (2.0 * e);\\n      vec2 normal = normalize(vec2(-slope, 1.0));\\n      vec2 toMoon = normalize(lakeMoon - vec2(p.x, top));\\n      float rib = snoise(vec2(p.x * 22.0, below * 3.0 + i));\\n      float lit = smoothstep(0.1, 0.8, dot(normal, toMoon) + 0.25 * rib);\\n      // Each nearer layer is darker: moonlit snow far, woods, then rock.\\n      vec3 shade = mix(vec3(0.12, 0.13, 0.27), vec3(0.03, 0.035, 0.08), i * 0.5);\\n      vec3 light = mix(vec3(0.78, 0.8, 0.92), vec3(0.2, 0.23, 0.3), i * 0.5);\\n      // Snow lies near the crests and gives way to dark rock below.\\n      float snow = 1.0 - smoothstep(0.03, 0.1, below + 0.03 * rib);\\n      light = mix(light * 0.35, light, mix(snow, 1.0, i * 0.5));\\n      vec3 face = mix(shade, light, lit);\\n      // A rim of moonlight along every crest on the lit side.\\n      face = mix(face, vec3(0.7, 0.72, 0.82), smoothstep(0.01, 0.0, below) * lit * 0.5);\\n      // Atmospheric perspective: far layers and every layer\'s foot sink\\n      // into the haze.\\n      float far = mix(0.3, 0.0, i * 0.5);\\n      far = mix(far, 1.0, 0.4 * (1.0 - smoothstep(0.0, 0.05, p.y - lakeHorizon)));\\n      col = mix(face, haze, far);\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return col;\\n}\\n\\nvec3 lakeWater(vec2 p, vec2 cellId) {\\n  float depth = (lakeHorizon - p.y) / lakeHorizon;\\n  // Mirror about the horizon, wobbled by ripples that grow toward the viewer.\\n  float wob = (0.002 + 0.01 * depth) * sin(p.y * 220.0 / (0.25 + depth) + lakeT * 0.5 + 2.0 * snoise(vec2(p.x * 2.0, p.y * 10.0)));\\n  vec3 col = lakeAbove(vec2(p.x + wob, 2.0 * lakeHorizon - p.y), cellId);\\n  col = col * vec3(0.6, 0.66, 0.8) * mix(0.95, 0.55, depth) + vec3(0.02, 0.03, 0.07);\\n  // The moon\'s path: glints in a column that widens toward the viewer.\\n  float dx = abs(p.x - lakeMoon.x) / lakeSqueeze;\\n  float width = 0.012 + 0.07 * depth;\\n  float column = exp(-dx * dx / (width * width));\\n  float glint = smoothstep(0.35, 0.85, snoise(vec2(p.x * 26.0, p.y * 130.0 + lakeT * 0.25)));\\n  col += vec3(0.9, 0.86, 0.72) * column * (0.12 + glint) * mix(1.0, 0.55, depth);\\n  return col;\\n}\\n\\nvec3 lakeColor(vec2 uv, vec2 res, float t) {\\n  float aspect = res.x / res.y;\\n  // Narrow boxes squeeze x, so a phone still sees the hills and the moon.\\n  lakeSqueeze = sqrt(clamp(1.6 / aspect, 1.0, 4.0));\\n  float portrait = 1.0 - smoothstep(0.7, 1.1, aspect);\\n  lakeT = t;\\n  lakeHalfW = 0.5 * aspect * lakeSqueeze;\\n  // On a phone the headline fills the middle: the horizon and the hills\\n  // drop below the buttons and the moon rises into the sky above the text.\\n  lakeHorizon = mix(0.4, 0.2, portrait);\\n  lakeScale = mix(1.0, 0.7, portrait);\\n  lakeMoon = vec2(0.55 * lakeHalfW, mix(lakeHorizon + 0.3, 0.8, portrait));\\n  vec2 p = vec2((uv.x - 0.5) * aspect * lakeSqueeze, uv.y);\\n  vec2 cellId = floor(uv * res);\\n\\n  vec3 col = p.y >= lakeHorizon ? lakeAbove(p, cellId) : lakeWater(p, cellId);\\n\\n  // A dark near shore in the bottom left: the foreground plane.\\n  float shore = 0.12 + 0.05 * fbm(vec2(p.x * 3.0, 5.0)) - 0.3 * smoothstep(-0.9, 0.2, p.x / lakeHalfW);\\n  if (p.y < shore) {\\n    float rocks = 0.5 + 0.5 * snoise(vec2(p.x * 14.0, p.y * 9.0));\\n    col = mix(vec3(0.03, 0.035, 0.07), vec3(0.12, 0.12, 0.2), rocks * 0.5);\\n    col = mix(col, vec3(0.4, 0.4, 0.5), smoothstep(0.012, 0.0, shore - p.y) * 0.6);\\n  }\\n\\n  // Low mist drifting along the far shore.\\n  float band = exp(-pow((p.y - lakeHorizon - 0.01) / 0.025, 2.0));\\n  float mist = smoothstep(0.4, 0.7, fbm(vec2(p.x * 1.5 - t * 0.012, p.y * 12.0))) * band;\\n  return mix(col, vec3(0.42, 0.4, 0.54), mist * 0.6);\\n}\\n\\n// field and colorField run in the same shader pass, so the second call for\\n// a sample reads the cached result instead of painting the scene again.\\nvec2 lakeCacheUv = vec2(-1.0);\\nfloat lakeCacheT = -1.0;\\nvec4 lakeCacheValue = vec4(0.0);\\n\\nvec4 lakeScene(vec2 uv, vec2 res, float t) {\\n  if (uv == lakeCacheUv && t == lakeCacheT) {\\n    return lakeCacheValue;\\n  }\\n  vec3 col = clamp(lakeColor(uv, res, t), 0.0, 1.0);\\n  float peak = max(col.r, max(col.g, col.b));\\n  // Value counts saturated colour as mid-tone, so blue shadow still draws.\\n  float value = max(dot(col, vec3(0.3, 0.55, 0.15)), 0.7 * peak);\\n  float tone = 0.1 + 0.9 * pow(value, 1.2);\\n  // The focal light draws full dots, not a dithered mix of the two largest.\\n  tone = max(tone, smoothstep(0.82, 0.92, value));\\n  // Small dots read darker than the light they stand for: lift dark ink.\\n  vec3 ink = col / max(peak, 1e-3) * pow(peak, 0.6);\\n  // Quiet darks: the faintest specks, in a colour just above the paper.\\n  float quiet = 1.0 - smoothstep(0.08, 0.28, value);\\n  ink = mix(ink, vec3(0.11, 0.12, 0.2), quiet * 0.55);\\n  tone *= 1.0 - 0.35 * quiet;\\n  // Below full intensity the middle band, where a headline sits, quiets most.\\n  float k = lakeIntensity;\\n  vec2 c = vec2((uv.x - 0.5) * res.x / res.y / 0.55, (uv.y - 0.52) / 0.15);\\n  float middle = exp(-dot(c, c)) * (1.0 - k);\\n  tone *= mix(0.72, 1.0, k) * (1.0 - 0.45 * middle);\\n  ink *= mix(0.5, 1.0, k) * (1.0 - 0.6 * middle);\\n  lakeCacheUv = uv;\\n  lakeCacheT = t;\\n  lakeCacheValue = vec4(ink, tone);\\n  return lakeCacheValue;\\n}\\n`;\\n\\nconst FIELD = `${SCENE}\\nfloat field(vec2 uv, vec2 res, float t) {\\n  return lakeScene(uv, res, t).a;\\n}\\n`;\\n\\nconst COLOR_FIELD = `${SCENE}\\nvec3 colorField(vec2 uv, vec2 res, float t) {\\n  return lakeScene(uv, res, t).rgb;\\n}\\n`;\\n\\n// The scene paints its own night, so modes that use its colours keep this\\n// paper in light and dark themes alike.\\nconst NIGHT = \\"#070a14\\";\\nconst PAINTED: ReadonlySet<ColorMode> = new Set<ColorMode>([\\n  \\"truecolor\\",\\n  \\"ansi16\\",\\n  \\"duotone\\",\\n]);\\n\\nexport interface MoonLakeProps extends Omit<AsciiSceneProps, \\"interactive\\"> {\\n  /** How strongly it draws, from 0 (faint, for text on top) to 1. Default 1. */\\n  intensity?: number;\\n  /** Square cells, for an even lattice of dots. Default `true`. */\\n  square?: boolean;\\n  /** Paper behind the dots. Default: the scene\'s night in painted modes. */\\n  paper?: AsciiColor;\\n}\\n\\nexport const MoonLake = ({\\n  charset = \\"dots\\",\\n  colorMode = \\"truecolor\\",\\n  cell = 7,\\n  square = true,\\n  dither = \\"bayer4\\",\\n  stillTime = 6,\\n  intensity = 1,\\n  paper,\\n  ...props\\n}: MoonLakeProps) => (\\n  <AsciiShader\\n    field={FIELD}\\n    colorField={COLOR_FIELD}\\n    uniforms={{ lakeIntensity: Math.min(1, Math.max(0, intensity)) }}\\n    charset={charset}\\n    colorMode={colorMode}\\n    paper={paper ?? (PAINTED.has(colorMode) ? NIGHT : undefined)}\\n    cell={cell}\\n    square={square}\\n    dither={dither}\\n    stillTime={stillTime}\\n    fallback={<div className=\\"absolute inset-0 bg-[#070a14]\\" />}\\n    {...props}\\n  />\\n);\\n```\\n\\nTo look at it while you work, render it full-bleed behind a sample headline, the way a hero would use it:\\n\\n```tsx\\n<section className=\\"relative isolate flex min-h-svh flex-col items-center justify-center gap-4 px-6 text-center text-white\\">\\n  <MoonLake intensity={0.75} className=\\"absolute inset-0 -z-10\\" />\\n  <h1 className=\\"max-w-xl text-4xl font-medium sm:text-6xl\\">Your headline</h1>\\n  <p className=\\"text-white/75\\">A line of supporting copy.</p>\\n  <div className=\\"flex gap-3\\">\\n    <a className=\\"rounded-full bg-white px-5 py-2 text-black\\">Primary</a>\\n    <a className=\\"rounded-full border border-white/30 px-5 py-2\\">Secondary</a>\\n  </div>\\n</section>\\n```\\n\\n## Do and don\'t\\n\\n| Do                                                                        | Don\'t                                                                      |\\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| Paint every cell; let the darkest areas be specks in a near-paper colour  | Leave flat empty paper, or cover sky and shadow in bright, even dots       |\\n| Save the largest, brightest dots for the light, its reflection, lit edges | Draw the whole subject at full size; texture then flattens the depth       |\\n| Make far layers smaller, lower in contrast and closer to the sky colour   | Give every depth the same dot size and contrast                            |\\n| Shade faces lit and shadowed, with ribs, ledges and ragged edges          | Flat uniform faces, stair-step contours, evenly spaced stripes             |\\n| Keep the middle of the frame calm, and expose `intensity`                 | Put the light, a bright edge or busy detail behind the headline            |\\n| Reframe for portrait so the subject sits beside the headline              | Let a phone crop hide the subject behind the text                          |\\n| Measure round things with the squeeze undone                              | Draw a sun or moon in squeezed units (it turns oval on a phone)            |\\n| Use your own `hash` for per-cell randomness over large areas              | Rely on `hash21` of whole cells over wide areas: it repeats every 50 cells |\\n| Size shapes in cells: 8 or more across for anything that must read        | Tiny features that blur into a grid or alias into noise                    |\\n| Give the sky and water mid-tones, even at night                           | A black sky over black water, so half the frame is empty                   |\\n| Keep landmarks true to their real silhouette and proportions              | Draw a landmark as an icon with one flat, bright face                      |\\n| Calm motion that a still frame survives                                   | Rain, beams, lightning, flicker, pointer effects                           |\\n\\n## Check it\\n\\n1. Screenshot at 1440x900 and at 390x844, bare and behind a sample headline and two buttons. Look at each honestly: can you name the place in a second? Is there one clear light? Do far layers sit back? Is the middle calm behind the text?\\n2. Zoom into a crop at 2x or more. You see round dots of several sizes on an even lattice, not stripes, not blur, not a flat block.\\n3. On the phone, the subject is recognisable beside the text, the light is round, and nothing bright sits behind the buttons.\\n4. Emulate `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`: the root reports `data-state=\\"static\\"` and the `stillTime` frame looks finished.\\n5. Toggle the site\'s light and dark themes: a painted scene looks the same in both, because it paints its own paper.\\n6. The console shows no `[ascii-shader]` compile errors or WebGL warnings. A compile error names the line; a black box with the fallback means the field didn\'t compile.\\n7. Then hold it to [quality.md](quality.md).\\n\\nIf it looks weak, the cause is usually one of: too dark (the subject has no bright values; check in `mono`), half empty (a black sky and black water), too small (features under 8 cells), too busy (dark areas aren\'t quiet), flat (no lit and shadow faces, no haze by depth), or diagrammatic (straight edges, even repeats).\\n\\n## Going further: a real 3D scene\\n\\nThe shipped backgrounds paint 2D layers, which is enough for most scenes. For a scene that must hold up to close inspection (a city with depth, a canyon you look into), raymarch it in the same `field`: model terrain and buildings as height fields and signed distance functions, cast rays from a camera with a field of view placed where a photographer would stand, light faces from the normal, march toward the light for cast shadows, reflect water by tracing the mirrored ray (`reflect(rd, normal)` with a slight ripple in the normal), and add fog that thickens exponentially with distance toward the sky colour. Keep it to 64 to 128 steps with bounding boxes, and animate only what moves on top of static geometry. The dot look, the tone mapping, the calm centre and the checks above all stay the same.\\n"},{"path":"skills/references/theme.md","language":"markdown","content":"# Theme and colour\\n\\n## The tokens\\n\\n`ascii-tokens` writes these into the project\'s global CSS, in `:root` and `.dark`:\\n\\n| Variable               | Default                    | What it controls                                                  |\\n| ---------------------- | -------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| `--ascii-ink`          | `var(--primary)`           | Glyph colour in `mono`, and the top of the default palette        |\\n| `--ascii-paper`        | `var(--background)`        | The surface behind the glyphs                                     |\\n| `--ascii-cell`         | `12px`                     | Glyph cell HEIGHT in CSS px. The width follows the font\'s advance |\\n| `--ascii-font`         | the project mono font      | The font glyphs are drawn in                                      |\\n| `--ascii-glow`         | about 0.35 light, 0.9 dark | Bloom strength, 0 to 1                                            |\\n| `--ascii-color-<name>` | tuned per theme            | `blue`, `purple`, `green`, `pink`, `orange`, `red`, `grey`        |\\n\\nTailwind gets matching utilities from `@theme inline`: `text-ascii-ink`, `bg-ascii-paper`, `text-ascii-blue` and so on.\\n\\n## Naming colours in props\\n\\nEvery colour prop takes an `AsciiColor`:\\n\\n- A palette name: `\\"ink\\"`, `\\"blue\\"`, `\\"purple\\"`, `\\"green\\"`, `\\"pink\\"`, `\\"orange\\"`, `\\"red\\"`, `\\"grey\\"`. These win over the CSS keywords they share.\\n- A CSS variable name: `\\"--chart-1\\"`, `\\"--ascii-paper\\"`.\\n- Any CSS colour: `\\"var(--accent)\\"`, `\\"oklch(0.7 0.15 272)\\"`, `\\"#fff\\"`.\\n\\nPrefer names and variables. They follow light and dark mode and a site palette switch on the next frame without a reload; literal colours don\'t.\\n\\n## Palettes\\n\\n`palette` is paper first, then inks from faint to strong:\\n\\n```tsx\\n<AsciiShader\\n  field={FIELD}\\n  colorMode=\\"palette\\"\\n  palette={[\\"--ascii-paper\\", \\"--chart-5\\", \\"--chart-1\\"]}\\n/>\\n```\\n\\nTwo entries (paper, ink) cover most effects. Three to five give depth: the faint ink colours sparse glyphs, the strong ink colours dense ones. For several lines or labels, give each its own colour with `seriesColor(undefined, i)` or the palette names.\\n\\n## Light and dark\\n\\nDark mode comes from the `.dark` class on `<html>`. The renderers watch that class and the `--ascii-*` variables and redraw when they change. To check dark mode in a browser, toggle the class (`document.documentElement.classList.toggle(\\"dark\\")`); the media query alone doesn\'t change shadcn themes.\\n\\n- Glyphs are thin. Keep 4.5:1 between ink and paper, like body text.\\n- Bloom (`fx.bloom`, scaled by `--ascii-glow`) reads in dark mode and washes out in light mode. Components should read `--ascii-glow` rather than a fixed bloom.\\n- A section that must always be dark (a terminal, a phosphor screen) wraps itself in `className=\\"dark\\"` so the tokens take their dark values there.\\n\\n## Overriding for one section\\n\\n```tsx\\n<section style={{ \\"--ascii-ink\\": \\"var(--chart-2)\\", \\"--ascii-cell\\": \\"16px\\" } as React.CSSProperties}>\\n```\\n\\nEverything inside draws in the new colour and cell size. Type the style as `React.CSSProperties & Record<\\"--ascii-ink\\" | \\"--ascii-cell\\", string>` if the project forbids casts.\\n"}]'),n="",t={files:e,prompt:n};export{t as default,e as files,n as prompt};
